var e={"infectious-fever-acute-undifferentiated-fever":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Fever** is an oral temperature of **38.0 C or more** from a raised hypothalamic set-point.`,`**Acute undifferentiated fever** is fever of **under 14 days with no localising focus**.`,`It is a working label that must be replaced by a cause within 5-7 days.`,`**Malaria, dengue, scrub typhus, enteric fever and leptospirosis** cause almost all the deaths.`,`AUF peaks after the monsoon, **July to November**, filling 20-40% of OPD attendances.`,`About **30-40% stay undiagnosed**; most are self-limiting viral fevers needing follow-up only.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify by syndrome: **fever plus what**.`,`Syndromes are fever alone, or with thrombocytopenia, rash, jaundice, altered sensorium, ARDS or AKI.`,`By duration: acute under 2 weeks, prolonged fever 2-3 weeks.`,`**Pyrexia of unknown origin** is over 38.3 C for over 3 weeks, undiagnosed after basic work-up.`,`Fever with shock, bleeding, breathlessness, drowsiness, oliguria or jaundice is **sepsis or severe tropical fever**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Microbial products (PAMPs) bind **Toll-like receptors** on monocytes and macrophages.`,`These cells release the pyrogens **IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha**.`,`In the OVLT, endothelial **COX-2** makes **PGE2**, which acts on hypothalamic EP3 receptors.`,`The set-point rises, so vasoconstriction, shivering and rigors push the core temperature up.`,`When the set-point falls, vasodilatation and sweating break the fever.`,`**Paracetamol** lowers brain PGE2 without blocking platelets, so it is safe in dengue.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Fix the **day of illness** and write it on every prescription.`,`Ask the fever pattern: abrupt with retro-orbital pain, rigors, step-ladder, or a second spike.`,`Ask exposure: grass for scrub, **flood water or paddy** for leptospirosis, travel for malaria.`,`Ask about cluster cases in the lane; report clusters under **IDSP**.`,`Ask for self-medication with antibiotics, NSAIDs, and any new drug in 3 weeks (drug fever).`,`Ask immune status, diabetes, steroids, chemotherapy, and the **LMP** in every woman aged 15-49.`,`Fever beyond 2 weeks with weight loss or cough moves **TB, HIV and kala-azar** up.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Screen for sepsis with **NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS**, not qSOFA alone (SSC 2026).`,`A pulse pressure of **20 mmHg or less** and cold peripheries mean compensated dengue shock.`,`**Undress and search for an eschar** in axilla, groin, waistband and under the breasts.`,`Look for **conjunctival suffusion** and calf tenderness in leptospirosis.`,`Rash on palms and soles suggests rickettsia; purpura suggests meningococcaemia, an emergency.`,`**Relative bradycardia (Faget sign)** points to enteric fever, leptospirosis or rickettsia.`,`**Repeat the examination daily**; eschar, rash and spleen often appear by day 4-5.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Day 1-3 panel: **CBC with haematocrit, malaria RDT plus smear, dengue NS1, urine**.`,`Send **two blood cultures before the first antibiotic** in toxic or prolonged fever.`,`Send **IgM ELISA from day 5**; an earlier IgM is a falsely reassuring negative.`,`Leucopenia suggests dengue, malaria or typhoid; neutrophilia suggests leptospirosis or sepsis.`,`In sepsis, a **lactate of 2 or more** is abnormal and 4 or more is high risk.`,`**Do not send a single Widal test**; it has no role in endemic India.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Give **paracetamol only** until dengue is excluded; no NSAIDs, aspirin or IM injections.`,`Advise oral fluids **2.5-3 L a day** and passing urine at least every 6 hours.`,`**Withhold antibiotics for 3 days** in a well patient without a focus.`,`After day 3-5, give **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days** for suspected scrub or lepto.`,`Use **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days** in pregnancy or doxycycline intolerance.`,`Give a **written safety-net** and review in **24-48 hours**, especially at defervescence.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Suspected enteric fever: **azithromycin 1 g once daily for 7 days**, or cefixime for 14 days.`,`Sepsis with no focus: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV plus doxycycline** within 1 hour, after cultures.`,`Sepsis fluids: **at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid**, Ringer lactate preferred over saline.`,`Start noradrenaline to keep **MAP 65 mmHg** if hypotension persists after fluids.`,`Dengue shock is different: titrated crystalloid **5-10 or 20 mL/kg**, not the sepsis block.`,`Use **meropenem** only for hospital-onset sepsis, ESBL carriage or failure at 48-72 hours.`,`**Do not start steroids** in undiagnosed fever; they mask TB, lymphoma and enteric fever.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Pregnancy: paracetamol is safe; use **azithromycin instead of doxycycline**.`,`Admit any pregnant woman with dengue or malaria.`,`Children: short doxycycline courses (21 days or less) **do not stain teeth**.`,`Liver disease or malnutrition: reduce paracetamol to **2-3 g a day**.`,`High-risk febrile neutropenia (MASCC under 21): **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly** within 1 hour.`,`Drowsy patient: **check capillary glucose first** and correct hypoglycaemia.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Admit for shock, bleeding, **SpO2 under 94%**, altered sensorium, oliguria or jaundice.`,`Repeat CBC with haematocrit **daily from day 3** if dengue is possible.`,`Scrub typhus settles **within 48 hours of doxycycline**; enteric fever within 5-7 days.`,`No response means wrong diagnosis, resistance or a complication; re-evaluate, do not escalate blindly.`,`Refer fever beyond 2 weeks without diagnosis for a structured PUO work-up.`,`**Notify** malaria, dengue, scrub, lepto, enteric clusters and JE/AES on the **IHIP** portal.`]}],tables:[{heading:`AUF drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day; child 15 mg/kg`,`Only while symptomatic`,`Liver toxicity; 2-3 g/day in liver disease`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral BD; child 2.2 mg/kg BD`,`7 days`,`Pill oesophagitis, photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin (scrub, rickettsia)`,`500 mg oral OD; child 10 mg/kg`,`5 days`,`Diarrhoea, QT prolongation; safe in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin (enteric)`,`1 g oral OD; child 20 mg/kg, max 1 g`,`7 days`,`Diarrhoea, QT prolongation`],[`Cefixime (enteric)`,`20 mg/kg/day oral in 2 doses`,`14 days`,`Diarrhoea; slower defervescence`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV OD; child 50-75 mg/kg/day`,`10-14 days`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; add doxycycline in sepsis`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly`,`As per culture`,`Only hospital-onset, ESBL or failure`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly`,`Until neutropenia recovers`,`High-risk febrile neutropenia, within 1 hour`]]},{heading:`Which test on which day`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`When`],rows:[[`Malaria RDT plus smear`,`Malaria, species and parasite density`,`Any day; repeat 12-hourly x 3 if suspicious`],[`Dengue NS1 antigen`,`Current dengue`,`Days 1-5`],[`Blood culture, 2 sets`,`Enteric fever or bacteraemia with sensitivity`,`Before any antibiotic, best in week 1`],[`IgM ELISA (dengue, scrub, lepto)`,`Recent infection`,`Day 5 onwards`],[`LFT, RFT, electrolytes, chest X-ray, lactate`,`Organ dysfunction, not the organism`,`Unwell or fever beyond 7 days`]]},{heading:`Fever plus what - syndromic differential`,columns:[`Syndrome`,`Think of`,`Key clue`],rows:[[`Fever with thrombocytopenia`,`Dengue, malaria, scrub, lepto, enteric, sepsis`,`Leucopenia versus leucocytosis`],[`Fever with rash`,`Dengue, chikungunya, spotted fever, meningococcaemia`,`Palms and soles - rickettsia`],[`Fever with jaundice`,`Falciparum, leptospirosis, hepatitis A and E`,`Bilirubin far above transaminases`],[`Fever with altered sensorium`,`Cerebral malaria, meningitis, JE/AES, scrub`,`Check glucose and sodium first`],[`Fever with breathlessness`,`Pneumonia, scrub ARDS, lepto lung haemorrhage`,`Haemoptysis - lepto; eschar - scrub`],[`Fever with AKI`,`Leptospirosis, falciparum, scrub, sepsis`,`Non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI - lepto`]]}],remember:[`Write the **day of illness** on every fever prescription.`,`Undress every febrile patient and **hunt the eschar**.`,`Doxycycline covers scrub, rickettsia and lepto; ceftriaxone covers none of them.`,`NS1 and smear early, IgM from day 5, culture before antibiotic.`,`Paracetamol only until dengue is excluded: no NSAIDs, no IM injections, no steroids.`,`Worsening as the fever settles means the **dengue critical phase**, not recovery.`,`Faget sign (relative bradycardia): enteric, lepto, rickettsia, brucella, drug fever.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`SSC 2026: screen for sepsis with NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS rather than qSOFA as a single tool.`,`SSC 2026: at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid (Ringer lactate over saline) guided by dynamic measures; MAP 65 (60-65 acceptable at 65 years or older); lactate repeated to guide resuscitation.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Acute undifferentiated fever`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Fever (oral **38.0 C or more**) of **under 14 days with no localising focus** after history, examination and bedside tests - a working label to be replaced by a cause in 5-7 days.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Syndromic - fever plus what**: alone, thrombocytopenia, rash, jaundice, altered sensorium, ARDS, AKI; by duration - acute under 2 weeks, prolonged 2-3 weeks, **PUO over 3 weeks**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Malaria, dengue, scrub typhus, enteric fever, leptospirosis** (plus influenza) - 60-70% of diagnosed cases; peaks July-November; 30-40% stay undiagnosed (mostly viral).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`PAMPs -> TLRs -> **IL-1, IL-6, TNF** -> OVLT COX-2 -> **PGE2 -> EP3** -> raised hypothalamic set-point -> vasoconstriction, rigors -> fever; paracetamol lowers PGE2 without platelet effect.`],[`Key history`,`**Day of illness**, fever pattern, focus symptoms, exposure (grass - scrub; floodwater - lepto; travel - malaria), cluster cases, drugs (NSAIDs, antibiotics), LMP, immune status, warning symptoms.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals with pulse pressure, SpO2, capillary refill; **undress and hunt the eschar**; conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness, rash, nodes, spleen, hidden foci; **relative bradycardia**; repeat daily.`],[`Investigations`,`Day 1-3: **CBC with haematocrit, malaria RDT plus smear, dengue NS1, urine**; **2 blood cultures before antibiotic**; **IgM ELISA from day 5**; lactate 2 or more abnormal, 4 or more high risk; **no single Widal**.`],[`Treatment - general`,`Oral fluids 2.5-3 L/day, urine at least 6-hourly, tepid sponging, **no IM injections**, written safety-net, **review in 24-48 h** especially at defervescence.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Paracetamol 500-1000 mg 6-hourly, max 4 g/day** (child 15 mg/kg); **no NSAIDs**; no antibiotic for 3 days if well; then **doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days** (child 2.2 mg/kg BD) or **azithromycin 500 mg OD x 5 days** in pregnancy.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Enteric suspected: **azithromycin 1 g OD x 7 days** or cefixime 20 mg/kg/day x 14 days; sepsis: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV + doxycycline** within 1 h; **meropenem 1 g 8-hourly** only for hospital-onset, ESBL or failure.`],[`Emergency`,`Sepsis: lactate, cultures, antibiotics within 1 h, **at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid**, noradrenaline for **MAP 65**; dengue shock - titrated 5-10 or 20 mL/kg, not the sepsis block; drowsy - **check glucose**.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy: paracetamol, azithromycin not doxycycline, admit dengue or malaria; child: doxycycline short course safe; febrile neutropenia (MASCC under 21) - **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g 6-hourly** within 1 h.`],[`Complications`,`Shock, bleeding, ARDS, AKI, encephalopathy, hepatitis, DIC - each points to a specific tropical fever (lepto AKI with low K; scrub ARDS; falciparum coma).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`SBP under 90 or pulse pressure 20 or less, bleeding, SpO2 under 94%, altered sensorium, oliguria, jaundice, purpura, pregnancy, infancy, neutropenia, **fever beyond 7 days without diagnosis**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review daily or 48-hourly; CBC daily from day 3 if dengue possible; **scrub settles within 48 h of doxycycline**, enteric in 5-7 days - non-response means wrong diagnosis or complication.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Mosquito protection, weekly dry day, safe water, TCV; **notify malaria, dengue, scrub, lepto, enteric clusters, JE/AES on IDSP-IHIP**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SSC 2026**: NEWS2/MEWS/SIRS over qSOFA for screening, balanced crystalloids and dynamic measures, MAP 65 (60-65 in older adults), prolonged beta-lactam infusion.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Write the day of illness; IgM before day 5 is a wasted negative; ceftriaxone covers none of scrub, malaria or dengue; Faget sign - enteric, lepto, rickettsia; paracetamol only till dengue excluded.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`AUF in the OPD: day-of-illness work-up`,steps:[{label:`Fix the day of illness; vitals and red flags`,detail:`Shock, bleeding, SpO2 under 94%, drowsy, oliguria, jaundice`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Any red flag - sepsis or severe tropical fever`,detail:`Cultures, antibiotics in 1 h, admit; NEWS2 not qSOFA`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Undress, search eschar and focus`,detail:`Axilla, groin, waist, under breasts; calves, eyes, spleen`},{label:`Day 1-3 panel`,detail:`CBC + haematocrit, malaria RDT + smear, NS1, urine`},{label:`Day 5 onwards add IgM ELISA`,detail:`Dengue MAC, scrub, leptospira; blood culture before antibiotic`},{label:`Well, no focus - paracetamol and review 24-48 h`,detail:`No antibiotic for 3 days; no NSAID, no IM injection`},{label:`Day 3-5 still febrile, tests negative`,detail:`Doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days (azithromycin if pregnant)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Afebrile within 48 h - cause treated`,detail:`Narrow therapy; notify on IHIP`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Empirical treatment ladder in AUF`,steps:[{label:`Paracetamol only`,detail:`500-1000 mg 6-hourly, max 4 g/day; child 15 mg/kg`},{label:`Doxycycline for scrub/lepto/rickettsia`,detail:`100 mg BD x 7 days; child 2.2 mg/kg BD`},{label:`Azithromycin - pregnancy or suspected enteric`,detail:`500 mg OD x 5 days (scrub); 1 g OD x 7 days (enteric)`},{label:`Sepsis with no focus`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD + doxycycline 100 mg BD within 1 hour`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Hospital-onset, ESBL or failure at 72 h`,detail:`Meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly; re-culture`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Haemogram clues in Indian fever`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Think of`,`Discriminator`],rows:[[`Leucopenia + low platelets`,`Dengue, malaria, enteric`,`NS1, smear, blood culture`],[`Neutrophilia + low platelets`,`Leptospirosis, scrub, sepsis`,`Eschar, calf tenderness, low K AKI`],[`Bilirubin far above transaminases`,`Leptospirosis, falciparum`,`MAT/IgM, smear`],[`Transaminases 100-500, little jaundice`,`Dengue, scrub, typhoid`,`Day of illness, eschar`]]}]},"infectious-fever-dengue":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Dengue** is a flavivirus fever with **four serotypes (DENV 1-4)**.`,`It is spread by the day-biting **Aedes aegypti** mosquito.`,`Incubation is **4-10 days**, so fever starting over 14 days after exposure is not dengue.`,`The illness has **febrile, critical and recovery phases**.`,`Patients die of **plasma leak around defervescence**, not of the platelet count.`,`India's cases peak **August to November**; deaths stay under 1% with good fluid care.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Use the **WHO 2009 classification**, kept in WHO 2025 and NCVBDC guidelines.`,`Three grades: **without warning signs, with warning signs, and severe dengue**.`,`Severe dengue means **shock or breathlessness from leak, severe bleeding, or organ failure**.`,`Organ involvement includes **AST or ALT 1000 or more**, encephalopathy, myocarditis or AKI.`,`Disposal groups: **A home, B admit, C emergency** care.`,`The old DF and DHF grades I-IV are historical only.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`The virus infects dendritic cells, monocytes and macrophages, then spreads to marrow and endothelium.`,`Cytokines cause the febrile phase: high fever, retro-orbital pain and **break-bone** myalgia.`,`Secreted **NS1** sheds the endothelial glycocalyx and makes capillaries leaky.`,`Leak lasts **24-48 hours around defervescence (day 3-7)** into pleura and peritoneum.`,`Haemoconcentration, effusions and falling preload narrow the pulse pressure, then cause shock.`,`**Antibody-dependent enhancement** in a second serotype explains why secondary dengue is more severe.`,`In recovery, leaked fluid returns, so continued IV fluid causes **pulmonary oedema**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Fix the **day of illness**: days 1-3 febrile, **days 3-7 critical**, then recovery.`,`Ask about abrupt high fever, severe headache, retro-orbital pain and body aches.`,`Ask every **warning-sign question** at every visit.`,`Ask about drinking, and whether urine has been passed in the **last 6 hours**.`,`Ask about NSAIDs, aspirin, steroids or IM injections already taken.`,`Ask about pregnancy, age over 60, comorbidity and **living alone**, which mean admission.`,`A previous dengue infection makes this one secondary, with higher risk.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **pulse pressure** at every visit; it is the earliest sign of shock.`,`**Compensated shock**: pulse pressure **20 mmHg or less**, capillary refill over 2 seconds, normal systolic BP.`,`**Hypotensive shock**: systolic BP below 90 mmHg or unrecordable; this is an emergency.`,`**Tourniquet test**: cuff for 5 minutes, positive with **10 or more petechiae per square inch**.`,`Look for tender liver over 2 cm, ascites and **right pleural effusion**.`,`In recovery look for fluid overload: puffy face, raised JVP and basal crackles.`,`Record **weight** at admission, because every fluid rate is in mL/kg.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**CBC with haematocrit** on day 1 as baseline, then daily from day 3.`,`In Groups B and C repeat haematocrit **4-6 hourly**; the trend guides fluid.`,`**NS1 antigen on days 1-5** confirms current dengue.`,`**IgM MAC-ELISA from day 5**; free at NCVBDC sentinel hospitals.`,`Never diagnose current dengue on **IgG alone or a rapid combo card**.`,`Ultrasound shows effusion, ascites and **gall bladder wall over 3 mm**, the proof of leak.`,`Always send a **malaria smear or RDT**, as co-infection is common.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**There is no antiviral**; care is supportive.`,`Group A: oral fluids **2.5-3 L a day** with ORS, coconut water, soups and buttermilk.`,`**Paracetamol only**, 500-1000 mg 6-hourly, maximum 4 g a day.`,`**No NSAIDs, aspirin, steroids, IM injections or papaya leaf**.`,`Review **daily from day 3** until 48 hours after the fever settles.`,`Teach the family: **worsening as the fever settles is danger, not recovery**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Group B fluid: crystalloid **5-7, then 3-5, then 2-3 mL/kg/hour**, rechecking haematocrit.`,`Compensated shock: crystalloid **5-10 mL/kg over 1 hour**, then step down.`,`Hypotensive shock: **20 mL/kg crystalloid over 15-30 minutes**; WHO 2025 prefers crystalloid to colloid.`,`Rising haematocrit with shock means continuing leak: give a second crystalloid bolus.`,`**Falling haematocrit with shock means bleeding**: give whole blood or packed cells.`,`Platelets only for significant bleeding, or prophylactically **below 10,000** (NCVBDC).`,`Step fluids down early and **stop by 24-48 hours** to avoid overload.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Seven warning signs: **abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, fluid accumulation, mucosal bleeding**.`,`The other three: **lethargy or restlessness, liver over 2 cm, rising haematocrit with falling platelets**.`,`Unresponsive shock: correct **ABCS - acidosis, bleeding, calcium, sugar**.`,`Recovery overload: stop the drip; furosemide only once the leak has ended.`,`Expanded dengue syndrome: encephalopathy, myocarditis, liver failure, AKI or HLH.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Admit (Group B) for warning signs, pregnancy, infancy, age over 60, comorbidity or social risk.`,`Refer (Group C) for shock, severe bleeding, organ failure or SpO2 under 94%.`,`Give the first bolus and paracetamol before transfer.`,`Discharge: **48 hours afebrile** without antipyretic and stable haematocrit off fluids for 24 hours.`,`Also needed before discharge: good urine output and **platelets rising above 50,000**.`,`Prevention is **source reduction**: a weekly dry day, emptied coolers and covered tanks.`,`**TAK-003 (Qdenga)** approved July 2026 for ages **4-60**, two doses 3 months apart.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Dengue drugs and fluids at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`Adult 500-1000 mg oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day; child 10-15 mg/kg`,`Only when over 38.5 C`,`Liver toxicity; 3 g/day in liver disease`],[`ORS and oral fluids`,`Adult 2.5-3 L/day`,`Through the critical phase`,`Vomiting if given too fast`],[`Crystalloid, Group B`,`IV 5-7, then 3-5, then 2-3 mL/kg/h`,`Stop by 24-48 h`,`Overload; titrate to haematocrit`],[`Crystalloid, shock`,`5-10 mL/kg over 1 h, or 20 mL/kg over 15-30 min`,`Then step down`,`Reassess after every bolus`],[`Colloid (dextran, gelatin, albumin)`,`10-20 mL/kg over 30-60 min`,`Refractory shock only`,`Allergy, coagulopathy, renal injury`],[`Whole blood or packed cells`,`Whole blood 10-20 mL/kg; PRBC 5-10 mL/kg`,`Once, then reassess`,`For falling haematocrit with shock`],[`Platelets`,`1 unit per 10 kg`,`Bleeding, or below 10,000`,`No benefit when given for a number`],[`Furosemide`,`0.5-1 mg/kg IV`,`Recovery overload only`,`Precipitates shock if leak continues`]]},{heading:`Dengue groups A, B and C`,columns:[`Feature`,`Group A`,`Group B`,`Group C`],rows:[[`Who`,`No warning sign, drinking well`,`Warning sign, comorbidity or social risk`,`Severe dengue`],[`Where`,`Home, daily review from day 3`,`Admit`,`Emergency, HDU or ICU`],[`Fluid`,`Oral fluids and ORS`,`Crystalloid 5-7, 3-5, 2-3 mL/kg/h`,`Bolus by shock type`],[`Monitoring`,`Daily CBC and warning signs`,`Haematocrit 4-6 hourly`,`Vitals every 15-30 min`]]},{heading:`Dengue tests`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`When`],rows:[[`NS1 antigen`,`Current dengue`,`Days 1-5`],[`IgM MAC-ELISA`,`Recent dengue`,`Day 5 onwards`],[`RT-PCR`,`Confirms and gives serotype`,`Days 1-5, reference use`],[`IgG or combo card`,`Past infection only`,`Not for confirmation`],[`Serial haematocrit`,`Leak if rising 20% or more`,`Daily from day 3`]]}],remember:[`Critical phase starts **as the fever falls**.`,`People die of leak, not of the platelet count.`,`Shock with **falling haematocrit = bleeding**: give blood, not crystalloid.`,`Never transfuse platelets for a number alone.`,`NS1 for days 1-5, IgM from day 5; never IgG alone.`,`Unresponsive shock, think **ABCS**: acidosis, bleeding, calcium, sugar.`,`Paracetamol only: no NSAIDs, steroids, IM injections or papaya leaf.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`CDSCO July 2026: TAK-003 (Qdenga) approved as India's first dengue vaccine, ages 4-60, two doses 3 months apart (private sector).`,`WHO 2025 arboviral guideline: crystalloid rather than colloid for IV fluid; capillary refill time to guide fluid; colloid kept only for refractory shock.`,`WHO 2025: suggests against prophylactic platelets in severe dengue with count under 50,000 and no bleeding (NCVBDC threshold under 10,000 retained).`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Dengue`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute febrile illness from **dengue virus (flavivirus, DENV 1-4)** spread by day-biting **Aedes aegypti**; incubation **4-10 days**; confirmed by **NS1, IgM seroconversion or RT-PCR**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**DENV 1-4**; Aedes aegypti breeds in clean stored water (coolers, tyres, pots); **secondary infection with a new serotype (ADE)**, infancy, pregnancy, over 60, obesity, diabetes, CKD, heart disease; **peak August-November**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**WHO 2009 (retained 2025)**: dengue without warning signs, with warning signs, **severe dengue**; disposal **Group A home, B admit, C emergency**; old DHF I-IV still in programme documents.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - 7 warning signs`,`**Abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, fluid accumulation, mucosal bleeding, lethargy/restlessness, liver over 2 cm, rising haematocrit with rapid platelet fall**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Secondary infection -> **ADE via Fc-gamma receptors** -> cytokines + **NS1 sheds glycocalyx** -> capillary leak at **defervescence (day 3-7)** for 24-48 h -> haemoconcentration -> narrow pulse pressure -> shock -> bleeding/DIC.`],[`Key history`,`**Day of illness**, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, warning-sign questions, oral intake and **urine in last 6 h**, NSAID or IM injection use, comorbidity, pregnancy, social risk, past dengue.`],[`Key examination`,`Pulse, **pulse pressure (20 or less = compensated shock)**, capillary refill over 2 s, tender liver, effusion, ascites, bleeding; tourniquet test 10 petechiae per sq inch; weight for fluid.`],[`Investigations`,`**CBC + haematocrit baseline, daily from day 3** (4-6-hourly in B and C); **NS1 day 1-5, IgM MAC-ELISA from day 5**; no IgG or combo cards; USG for effusion, GB wall over 3 mm.`],[`Treatment - Group A`,`Oral fluids **2.5-3 L/day**, **paracetamol 500-1000 mg 6-hourly (max 4 g)**, child 10-15 mg/kg; **no NSAIDs, steroids, IM injections, papaya leaf**; daily review from day 3.`],[`Treatment - Group B fluids`,`Crystalloid **5-7 mL/kg/h x 1-2 h, then 3-5 x 2-4 h, then 2-3 mL/kg/h**, titrated to haematocrit; stop by 24-48 h.`],[`Treatment - escalation (shock)`,`Compensated: **5-10 mL/kg over 1 h**; hypotensive: **20 mL/kg over 15-30 min**; rising Hct - another crystalloid bolus (colloid only refractory); **falling Hct - blood (whole blood 10-20 or PRBC 5-10 mL/kg)**.`],[`Blood products`,`**Platelets not for a number**: bleeding, or prophylactic under 10,000 (NCVBDC); WHO 2025 against prophylaxis under 50,000 without bleeding; FFP for coagulopathy with bleeding.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy - admit**, compare with own baseline Hct; infants, over 60, diabetes, CKD, heart disease, obesity = Group B; use **ideal body weight** in the obese.`],[`Complications`,`Shock, severe bleeding, **AST/ALT 1000 or more**, encephalopathy, myocarditis, AKI, HLH; **fluid overload in recovery** (stop fluids, furosemide only after leak ends).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any warning sign, pulse pressure 20 or less, SBP under 90, Hct rise 20% or more, bleeding, SpO2 under 94%, pregnancy - admit or refer after first bolus.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Discharge: **afebrile 48 h without antipyretic**, stable Hct off fluids 24 h, good urine, **platelets rising above 50,000**; post-dengue fatigue for weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Weekly dry day**, source reduction, day-time repellent, bed net in viraemic first 5 days; notify on IHIP; **Qdenga 2 doses 3 months apart, 4-60 years** (CDSCO 2026, not in UIP).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2025** arboviral guideline: crystalloid over colloid, CRT-guided fluids, no steroids or immunoglobulin, no prophylactic platelets; **Qdenga approved in India July 2026**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Worsening at defervescence = leak, not recovery; people die of leak, not platelets; shocked with falling Hct = bleeding, give blood; NS1 early, IgM after day 5.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Dengue: from first visit to disposal`,steps:[{label:`Probable dengue - fix day of illness`,detail:`NS1 day 1-5, CBC + Hct baseline`},{label:`Warning sign, comorbidity or social risk?`,detail:`7 warning signs; pregnancy, infant, over 60, CKD`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No - Group A home care`,detail:`ORS 2.5-3 L/day, paracetamol; daily CBC from day 3`,tone:`good`},{label:`Yes - Group B admit`,detail:`Crystalloid 5-7 -> 3-5 -> 2-3 mL/kg/h; Hct 4-6-hourly`},{label:`Shock, severe bleeding or organ failure?`,detail:`Pulse pressure 20 or less, CRT over 2 s, SBP under 90`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Group C - resuscitate`,detail:`5-10 mL/kg/h compensated; 20 mL/kg in 15-30 min hypotensive`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Recovery: stop fluids by 48 h`,detail:`Discharge: afebrile 48 h, stable Hct, platelets over 50,000`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Persisting dengue shock - read the haematocrit`,steps:[{label:`First crystalloid bolus`,detail:`20 mL/kg over 15-30 min (hypotensive); oxygen`},{label:`Improved - step down`,detail:`10 mL/kg/h x 1 h, then 5-7, 3-5, 2-3 mL/kg/h`,tone:`good`},{label:`Still shocked, Hct rising`,detail:`Second 10-20 mL/kg crystalloid; colloid only if refractory`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Still shocked, Hct falling`,detail:`Occult bleeding: whole blood 10-20 or PRBC 5-10 mL/kg`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Refractory - correct ABCS, ICU`,detail:`Acidosis, bleeding, calcium, sugar; inotrope after volume`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"infectious-fever-malaria":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Malaria** is Plasmodium infection spread by the **female Anopheles** mosquito.`,`India has almost only **P. vivax and P. falciparum**, roughly half each.`,`Incubation is about **12 days for falciparum** and **14 days for vivax**.`,`Programme rule: **test, treat, track**; never treat without an RDT or smear.`,`India aims for **zero indigenous cases by 2027** and elimination by 2030.`,`Classify as **uncomplicated or severe**; any one WHO criterion makes it severe.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Sporozoites multiply silently in the **liver** for 6-15 days.`,`Vivax leaves dormant **hypnozoites** that cause relapse; only primaquine kills them.`,`Merozoites invade red cells; **schizont rupture every 48 hours** causes the paroxysm.`,`Vivax invades only **reticulocytes (Duffy)**, so parasitaemia stays under 2%.`,`Falciparum **PfEMP1** sticks red cells to ICAM-1, CD36 and placental CSA.`,`This **sequestration** in brain, kidney and placenta causes severe disease.`,`Artesunate kills young rings **before they sequester**, so it saves more lives than quinine.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about fever with chills and rigors, but **test every fever** in endemic areas.`,`Ask about travel or residence in a malarious area, forest, mine or migrant work.`,`Ask about past malaria and whether the **14-day primaquine** was completed.`,`Ask danger questions: drowsiness, fits, **prostration**, dark urine, jaundice, breathlessness.`,`Ask the **LMP** in every woman aged 15-49; pregnancy changes the drug.`,`Ask about **G6PD deficiency** or past dark urine with drugs.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record GCS (Blantyre in children) and **capillary glucose** in anyone drowsy.`,`Look for pallor, icterus and a soft tender **spleen**.`,`Test for **prostration**: unable to sit or feed unaided.`,`Look for acidotic breathing, crackles and hypoxia; these are lethal markers.`,`**Cerebral malaria has no neck stiffness**; meningism means look for meningitis.`,`Check for **cola-coloured urine** (blackwater) and record urine output.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bivalent RDT** (HRP2 plus pan-pLDH) gives a result in 15-20 minutes.`,`**Thick and thin smear** is the gold standard: detection, species and parasite count.`,`If negative with high suspicion, **repeat the smear 12-hourly for 48 hours**.`,`**Never judge cure with an HRP2 RDT**; it stays positive 2-4 weeks.`,`In the unwell, check Hb, glucose, creatinine, bilirubin and lactate.`,`Test **G6PD** before 14-day primaquine where available.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Vivax**: chloroquine **25 mg/kg over 3 days** (10, 10, 5 mg/kg).`,`Plus **primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days** for radical cure.`,`**Falciparum**: artesunate 3 days **plus SP** on day 1 (ACT-SP).`,`In the **North-East**, use **artemether-lumefantrine** because of SP resistance.`,`Add **single-dose primaquine 0.75 mg/kg** for falciparum to block transmission.`,`**Mixed infection**: ACT as for falciparum plus 14-day primaquine.`,`Give the first dose under observation; repeat any dose vomited within 30 minutes.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Severe malaria: IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg** at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then daily.`,`Use **3 mg/kg per dose** in children under 20 kg.`,`Always follow with a **full 3-day oral ACT**; never end on artesunate alone.`,`If artesunate is unavailable: IM artemether, arteether, or IV quinine with a loading dose.`,`Pre-referral: **IM artesunate**, or **rectal artesunate 10 mg/kg** in children under 6.`,`Give **restrictive fluids**; large boluses cause pulmonary oedema.`,`Avoid steroids, mannitol and heparin in cerebral malaria.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Pregnancy, first-trimester falciparum: NCVBDC uses **quinine 7 days**; WHO also allows AL.`,`Pregnancy, second and third trimesters: **ACT** as for other adults.`,`**Severe malaria in pregnancy**: IV artesunate in all trimesters.`,`Vivax in pregnancy: chloroquine; **defer primaquine** until after breastfeeding.`,`No primaquine under 1 year; in G6PD deficiency give **0.75 mg/kg weekly for 8 weeks**.`,`Travellers: **doxycycline 100 mg daily** short stay, or **mefloquine 250 mg weekly** long stay.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Hypoglycaemia**, especially in children, pregnancy and on quinine; check glucose 4-hourly.`,`Treat glucose under 40 mg/dL with **25% dextrose 2 mL/kg** in children.`,`**Transfuse** for Hb under 7 g/dL in adults or under 5 g/dL in children.`,`Lactic acidosis predicts death better than coma.`,`**Severe vivax is real**: it can cause ARDS, AKI, anaemia and splenic rupture.`,`**Post-artesunate delayed haemolysis** appears 1-3 weeks later; check Hb on days 7, 14, 28.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Parasites should fall **75% or more by 48 hours** on ACT.`,`Follow-up smears on **days 3, 7, 14 and 28** detect recrudescence.`,`Day-3 positivity over 10% of baseline suggests **artemisinin partial resistance**.`,`Supervise primaquine through the **ASHA** and watch for dark urine.`,`Notify every case; the team does an **active fever survey** around it.`,`Prevention: **LLIN** every night, indoor residual spraying and larvivorous fish.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Malaria drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Chloroquine (vivax)`,`Oral 10, 10, 5 mg base/kg; adult 600, 600, 300 mg`,`3 days`,`Nausea, itching; safe in pregnancy`],[`Primaquine (vivax)`,`Oral 0.25 mg/kg daily; adult 15 mg`,`14 days`,`G6PD haemolysis; not pregnancy, lactation, under 1 year`],[`Primaquine (falciparum)`,`Oral 0.75 mg/kg; adult 45 mg`,`Single dose, day 2`,`Blocks transmission; not pregnancy or under 1 year`],[`Artesunate + SP (ACT-SP)`,`AS 4 mg/kg daily; SP 25/1.25 mg/kg day 1`,`3 days`,`SP rash; avoid SP in first trimester`],[`Artemether-lumefantrine`,`Oral twice daily by weight band`,`3 days (6 doses)`,`Take with fatty food or milk; North-East`],[`Artesunate IV or IM`,`2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h, then daily`,`At least 24 h, then full ACT`,`Delayed haemolysis at 1-3 weeks`],[`Quinine IV`,`20 mg salt/kg loading, then 10 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`7 days total with doxycycline`,`Hypoglycaemia, cinchonism, QT prolongation`],[`Doxycycline (prophylaxis)`,`100 mg oral daily`,`2 days before to 4 weeks after`,`Photosensitivity; not in pregnancy`],[`Mefloquine (prophylaxis)`,`250 mg oral weekly`,`2 weeks before to 4 weeks after`,`Neuropsychiatric effects; avoid in epilepsy`]]},{heading:`Vivax versus falciparum`,columns:[`Feature`,`P. vivax`,`P. falciparum`],rows:[[`Red cells invaded`,`Reticulocytes only; under 2%`,`All ages; can exceed 10%`],[`Hypnozoites`,`Yes, relapse`,`No`],[`Sequestration`,`Minimal`,`Brain, kidney, placenta`],[`Smear clues`,`Large cells, Schuffner dots`,`Multiple rings, banana gametocytes`],[`Treatment in India`,`Chloroquine plus 14-day primaquine`,`ACT-SP (AL in North-East) plus single primaquine`]]},{heading:`WHO severe malaria criteria`,columns:[`Domain`,`Criterion`],rows:[[`Neurological`,`GCS under 11, prostration, over 2 convulsions in 24 h`],[`Metabolic`,`Glucose under 40 mg/dL; lactate 5 mmol/L or more`],[`Blood`,`Hb 7 g/dL or less adult, 5 or less child; bleeding`],[`Kidney and liver`,`Creatinine over 3 mg/dL; bilirubin over 3 mg/dL`],[`Heart and lung`,`Pulmonary oedema, SpO2 under 92%; SBP under 80 mmHg`],[`Parasite load`,`Falciparum over 10% (over 5% in travellers)`]]}],remember:[`**Test before you treat**: bivalent RDT or smear first.`,`Chloroquine clears vivax blood; only **14-day primaquine** stops relapse.`,`ACT-SP everywhere except the **North-East**, where it is AL.`,`AL needs **fatty food or milk** for absorption.`,`Severe malaria: **IV artesunate**, then always a full oral ACT.`,`No primaquine in pregnancy, lactation, infancy or severe G6PD deficiency.`,`Cerebral malaria has no neck stiffness.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO 2024-2025 malaria guideline: after a near-patient G6PD test, high-dose primaquine (1 mg/kg/day x 7 days) or single-dose tafenoquine 300 mg with chloroquine are radical-cure options (not yet NCVBDC policy).`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Malaria`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Infection with **Plasmodium** spread by the **female Anopheles**; India has mainly **P. vivax and P. falciparum**; **test before treat** (bivalent RDT or smear).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Uncomplicated vs severe** (any one WHO criterion, falciparum or vivax); cases classed **indigenous, imported, introduced, induced, relapsing**; goal zero indigenous cases 2027, elimination 2030.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Falciparum belt - Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, MP, North-East; vectors An. culicifacies (rural), **An. stephensi (urban)**, fluviatilis/minimus (forest); migrants, forest and mine workers, pregnancy, under 5.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Sporozoite -> liver (vivax **hypnozoites** -> relapse) -> RBC cycle 48 h -> haemozoin, TNF -> paroxysm; falciparum **PfEMP1 cytoadherence** -> sequestration -> cerebral, renal, placental malaria; artesunate kills rings before sequestration.`],[`Key history`,`Fever with rigors, travel or residence in last month (1 year for vivax), past malaria and **primaquine completion**, pregnancy (LMP), G6PD history, danger symptoms (drowsy, prostration, dark urine, breathlessness).`],[`Key examination`,`Pallor, icterus, splenomegaly, **prostration**, GCS/Blantyre, acidotic breathing, SpO2, urine colour, bleeding, shock; **no neck stiffness** in cerebral malaria.`],[`Investigations`,`**Bivalent RDT** (HRP2 + pan-pLDH) or **thick and thin smear** (gold standard); repeat 12-hourly x 48 h if negative; Hb, platelets, glucose, creatinine, bilirubin, lactate; **G6PD before 14-day primaquine**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - severe malaria`,`GCS under 11 / Blantyre under 3, prostration, **more than 2 convulsions in 24 h**, **glucose under 40 mg/dL**, lactate 5 or more, **Hb 7 or less adult (5 or less child under 12) with parasites over 10,000/microlitre**, creatinine over 3, bilirubin over 3 (parasites over 100,000), pulmonary oedema, SBP under 80, **parasitaemia over 10%**.`],[`Treatment - vivax`,`**Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days (10-10-5)**, adult 600-600-300 mg; **primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily x 14 days** (adult 15 mg); G6PD deficient **0.75 mg/kg weekly x 8 weeks**.`],[`Treatment - falciparum`,`**ACT-SP**: artesunate 4 mg/kg x 3 days + SP 25/1.25 mg/kg day 1 (adult AS 200 mg + SP 1500/75); **North-East: AL 20/120 (adult 4 tablets) BD x 3 days with fatty food**; + **single-dose primaquine 0.75 mg/kg (45 mg)** day 2.`],[`Severe malaria`,`**IV/IM artesunate 2.4 mg/kg (3 mg/kg if under 20 kg) at 0, 12, 24 h then daily**, minimum 24 h, then **full oral ACT**; alternatives artemether, arteether, quinine 20 mg/kg load; pre-referral rectal artesunate 10 mg/kg.`],[`Supportive care`,`Glucose 4-hourly (**25% dextrose 2 mL/kg** child), restrictive fluids (FEAST), transfuse Hb under 7 adult / under 5 child, dialysis for AKI, ceftriaxone in shock; **no steroids, mannitol, heparin**.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy: **first trimester quinine (NCVBDC) or AL (WHO 2022)**, ACT in 2nd-3rd; chloroquine safe; **no primaquine in pregnancy, lactation, infants under 1 year**; IV artesunate in all trimesters.`],[`Complications`,`Cerebral malaria, hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis, AKI, **blackwater fever**, ARDS, severe anaemia, **post-artesunate delayed haemolysis (1-3 weeks)**, splenic rupture (vivax).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any severity criterion, vomiting all doses, pregnancy with falciparum, infant, high parasitaemia, no response by 48-72 h - IV artesunate and refer.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Smear **day 3** (positivity suggests artemisinin partial resistance), days 7, 14, 28; **Hb day 7, 14, 28** after IV artesunate; ASHA-supervised primaquine.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**LLIN**, IRS, larvivorous fish, source reduction; **NFME 2016-2030, NSP 2023-27**; traveller prophylaxis doxycycline 100 mg daily (2 days before to 4 weeks after) or mefloquine 250 mg weekly.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2024-25**: G6PD near-patient tests, **7-day high-dose primaquine (1 mg/kg/day)** and **tafenoquine 300 mg single dose** for vivax radical cure; single low-dose primaquine 0.25 mg/kg for falciparum.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`HRP2 RDT stays positive 2-4 weeks - not a test of cure; AL needs fat; never finish with artesunate monotherapy; skipping primaquine guarantees relapse.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Fever in an endemic area: test, treat, track`,steps:[{label:`Bivalent RDT or smear before any drug`,detail:`Negative but suspicious - repeat smear 12-hourly x 48 h`},{label:`Any severity criterion?`,detail:`Prostration, GCS under 11, glucose under 40, Hb 7 or less, SBP under 80`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Severe - IV artesunate now`,detail:`2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily; then full ACT`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Uncomplicated vivax`,detail:`Chloroquine 10-10-5 mg/kg + primaquine 0.25 mg/kg x 14 days`},{label:`Uncomplicated falciparum`,detail:`ACT-SP (AL in North-East) + primaquine 0.75 mg/kg day 2`},{label:`Smear day 3, 7, 14, 28; notify`,detail:`Active fever survey around the case`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Vivax vs falciparum - what changes the treatment`,columns:[`Feature`,`P. vivax`,`P. falciparum`],rows:[[`Hypnozoites`,`Yes - relapse`,`No`],[`Primaquine`,`0.25 mg/kg x 14 days (radical cure)`,`0.75 mg/kg single dose (gametocytes)`],[`Blood-stage drug`,`Chloroquine 3 days`,`ACT-SP; AL in North-East`],[`Sequestration`,`Minimal`,`PfEMP1 - cerebral, placental`],[`Parasitaemia`,`Under 2% (reticulocytes)`,`Can exceed 10%`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Severe malaria: drug ladder by availability`,steps:[{label:`IV or IM artesunate (first choice)`,detail:`2.4 mg/kg 0, 12, 24 h then daily; 3 mg/kg under 20 kg`,tone:`good`},{label:`Pre-referral at PHC`,detail:`IM artesunate, or rectal artesunate 10 mg/kg under 6 years`},{label:`Artemether or arteether IM`,detail:`Artemether 3.2 then 1.6 mg/kg; arteether 150 mg x 3 days`},{label:`Quinine if nothing else`,detail:`20 mg/kg over 4 h then 10 mg/kg 8-hourly; glucose checks`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Always complete with oral ACT`,detail:`Full 3-day course once able to swallow`}]}]},"infectious-fever-enteric-fever":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Enteric fever** is systemic infection with **Salmonella Typhi or Paratyphi A, B, C**.`,`Humans are the only reservoir; spread is by **faecally contaminated water and food**.`,`Incubation is **7-14 days**; peak age is **2-15 years**.`,`**Paratyphi A causes 20-25%** of Indian cases.`,`Untreated mortality is 10-20%, falling to **under 1%** with the right antibiotic.`,`A **chronic carrier** excretes Typhi for over 12 months, usually from a stone-filled gall bladder.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Clinically: **uncomplicated versus complicated**; this decides oral or IV treatment.`,`Complicated means bleeding, perforation, encephalopathy, shock, myocarditis or jaundice.`,`**MDR**: resistant to chloramphenicol, ampicillin and cotrimoxazole.`,`**Fluoroquinolone non-susceptibility** is near universal in India.`,`**XDR**: MDR plus fluoroquinolone and ceftriaxone resistance, from Pakistan.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Typhi crosses the ileum through **M cells over Peyer patches**.`,`The **Vi capsule** hides it from the immune system, so diarrhoea is uncommon.`,`It survives **inside macrophages** and spreads to liver, spleen and marrow.`,`A sustained secondary bacteraemia causes the **step-ladder fever** of week 1.`,`Bile re-seeds the Peyer patches, which ulcerate in week 2-3.`,`Ileal ulcers lie **along the long axis on the antimesenteric border** and perforate in **week 3**.`,`Longitudinal ulcers rarely stricture, unlike transverse tuberculous ulcers.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about fever **rising day by day** in week 1, with headache and dry cough.`,`Adults often have **constipation**; young children and HIV patients get diarrhoea.`,`Ask about unsafe water, street food and a **household or canteen cluster**.`,`Ask about **travel to Pakistan**, which suggests XDR typhoid.`,`Ask about antibiotics already taken; they lower culture yield.`,`Ask about occupation: **food handlers** must stay off work until cleared.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`A toxic, apathetic patient with a **coated tongue and clean red edges**.`,`**Relative bradycardia (Faget sign)**: pulse lower than expected for the fever.`,`**Rose spots** on the trunk in 5-30%.`,`Soft, mildly tender **splenomegaly** and hepatomegaly.`,`Examine daily in week 3 for **peritonism and loss of liver dullness**.`,`Muttering delirium or **coma vigil** marks severe typhoid.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Blood culture before the first antibiotic** is the key test.`,`Blood culture is positive in **60-80% in week 1**, falling by week 3.`,`**Bone marrow culture** is positive in **80-95%**, even after antibiotics.`,`CBC shows low or normal WBC with **aneosinophilia** and mild thrombocytopenia.`,`**Never treat on a single Widal**; it needs a fourfold rise on paired sera.`,`Typhidot and Tubex are only 70-80% accurate; never the sole proof.`,`Pefloxacin or nalidixic acid resistance predicts **fluoroquinolone failure**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Culture first, then treat by severity; **no empirical fluoroquinolones** in India.`,`Uncomplicated: **azithromycin 1 g once daily for 7 days** (child 20 mg/kg).`,`Oral alternative: **cefixime 20 mg/kg/day** in two doses for **14 days**.`,`Complicated or admitted: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV** once or twice daily for **10-14 days**.`,`Switch to oral azithromycin or cefixime once afebrile to finish the course.`,`Give a soft high-calorie diet; avoid NSAIDs, purgatives and enemas.`,`Pregnancy: **ceftriaxone or azithromycin** are safe; avoid fluoroquinolones.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Defervescence takes 3-5 days**; do not switch drugs on day 2.`,`Slow response on ceftriaxone: re-culture, look for abscess, and **add azithromycin**.`,`XDR or Pakistan travel: **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly plus azithromycin**.`,`Severe typhoid with delirium or shock: **high-dose dexamethasone** for 48 hours.`,`Perforation: resuscitate, NG tube, ceftriaxone plus metronidazole, and **early surgery**.`,`**Relapse** in 5-10% at 1-3 weeks: re-culture and re-treat.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Admit for peritonism, GI bleeding, altered sensorium, shock or persistent vomiting.`,`Refer fever beyond **7 days on treatment** to look for abscess or resistance.`,`Food handlers return after **three negative stool cultures**.`,`Notify clusters under **IDSP** so the water source can be traced.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**Safe water**, sanitation and handwashing are the lasting prevention.`,`**Typhoid conjugate vaccine**: single 0.5 mL IM dose from **6 months**.`,`TCV gives about 80% protection for at least 4-5 years.`,`Vi polysaccharide vaccine works only **from 2 years** and needs a booster every 3 years.`,`**No vaccine covers Paratyphi A**.`,`Chronic carrier: **4 weeks** of an effective drug; cholecystectomy if gallstones persist.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Enteric fever drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Azithromycin`,`1 g oral OD; child 20 mg/kg, max 1 g`,`7 days`,`GI upset, QT prolongation; first-line uncomplicated`],[`Cefixime`,`20 mg/kg/day oral in 2 doses; adult 200-400 mg BD`,`14 days`,`Diarrhoea; slower response, more relapse`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV OD or BD; child 75-100 mg/kg/day`,`10-14 days`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; complicated disease`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly; child 20-40 mg/kg`,`10-14 days, with azithromycin`,`XDR only; reduce in renal failure`],[`Dexamethasone`,`3 mg/kg IV, then 1 mg/kg 6-hourly`,`48 hours (8 doses)`,`Only delirium, coma or shock`],[`Ciprofloxacin (carrier)`,`750 mg oral BD`,`28 days`,`Only if pefloxacin susceptible; tendinopathy`]]},{heading:`Week by week`,columns:[`Week`,`Features`,`Best test`],rows:[[`Week 1`,`Step-ladder fever, headache, relative bradycardia`,`Blood culture`],[`Week 2`,`Toxaemia, rose spots, coated tongue, splenomegaly`,`Blood and marrow culture`],[`Week 3`,`Ileal bleeding, perforation, encephalopathy`,`Marrow culture; erect chest X-ray`],[`Week 4 on`,`Recovery; relapse 5-10%; carriage 1-4%`,`Stool and urine culture`]]},{heading:`Resistance types`,columns:[`Type`,`Resistant to`,`Use instead`],rows:[[`MDR`,`Chloramphenicol, ampicillin, cotrimoxazole`,`Azithromycin or ceftriaxone`],[`Fluoroquinolone non-susceptible`,`Ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin`,`Azithromycin or ceftriaxone`],[`Ceftriaxone resistant (ESBL)`,`Third-generation cephalosporins`,`Azithromycin or meropenem`],[`XDR`,`All of the above`,`Meropenem plus azithromycin`]]}],remember:[`**Culture before antibiotic**; a single Widal proves nothing.`,`Azithromycin for uncomplicated, **ceftriaxone for complicated**.`,`No empirical fluoroquinolones in India.`,`Fever takes **3-5 days** to settle on the right drug.`,`Faget sign, aneosinophilia and low WBC in week 2: enteric until proved otherwise.`,`Ulcers along the long axis: perforate in week 3, rarely stricture.`,`TCV from 6 months; nothing covers Paratyphi A.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Enteric fever`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Systemic infection with **Salmonella Typhi or Paratyphi A/B/C**, human-restricted, faeco-oral via water and food; **confirmed by culture** of blood, marrow, stool, urine.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Uncomplicated vs complicated** (bleeding, perforation, encephalopathy, shock, myocarditis, jaundice); resistance types **MDR, FQ non-susceptible (near universal in India), XDR** (Pakistan H58).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Unsafe water, street food, carrier cooks; peak **2-15 years**; Paratyphi A 20-25%; antacids/PPIs; gallstones for carriage; NSSEFI - hundreds to over 1000 per 100,000 child-years.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Ingestion -> M cells over **Peyer patches** (SPI-1) -> macrophage survival (SPI-2) -> RES -> secondary bacteraemia -> fever; **Vi capsule** masks LPS; week 3 longitudinal **antimesenteric ileal ulcers** bleed or perforate.`],[`Key history`,`**Step-ladder fever** week 1, headache, dry cough, constipation (adults), source/cluster, antibiotics taken, **Pakistan travel (XDR)**, vaccination, complication symptoms, food-handler occupation.`],[`Key examination`,`Toxic apathetic look, **coated tongue with clean edges**, **relative bradycardia**, rose spots (5-30%), soft splenomegaly, doughy abdomen; daily check for peritonism, melaena, delirium.`],[`Investigations`,`**Blood culture before antibiotic (10-15 mL adult), 60-80% positive week 1**; marrow culture 80-95% even after antibiotics; leucopenia, aneosinophilia; **no single Widal**; Typhidot only where culture unavailable.`],[`Treatment - general`,`Paracetamol, fluids, soft high-calorie diet; **no NSAIDs, purgatives, enemas**; enteric precautions; **food handlers off work until 3 negative stool cultures**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Uncomplicated: **azithromycin 1 g OD x 7 days** (child 20 mg/kg, max 1 g) or **cefixime 20 mg/kg/day in 2 doses x 14 days**; **no empirical fluoroquinolone**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Complicated: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD-BD (child 75-100 mg/kg/day, max 4 g) x 10-14 days**; slow response - add azithromycin; **XDR - meropenem 1 g 8-hourly + azithromycin**.`],[`Severe typhoid`,`Delirium, coma or shock: **dexamethasone 3 mg/kg IV then 1 mg/kg 6-hourly x 48 h (8 doses)** (Hoffman).`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - ceftriaxone or azithromycin, no FQ; children - azithromycin/cefixime by weight; renal - reduce meropenem only.`],[`Complications`,`Week 3 **ileal haemorrhage and perforation** (terminal ileum, within 60 cm of ICV), encephalopathy, myocarditis, hepatitis, cholecystitis, osteomyelitis; relapse 5-10%; **carriage 1-4%**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Peritonism or loss of liver dullness, melaena, delirium or shock, fever over 7 days on correct drug, XDR suspicion, infant or pregnancy.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Expect **defervescence in 3-5 days - do not switch on day 2**; relapse at 1-3 weeks - re-culture; stool culture at 1 month in food handlers.`],[`Carrier`,`Excretion over 12 months: **ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD x 28 days** if susceptible (or azithromycin/amoxicillin by sensitivity); **cholecystectomy** if gallstones.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Safe water and sanitation; **TCV single 0.5 mL IM from 6 months** (IAP 6-9 months; private only, not yet in UIP); Vi-PS from 2 years, 3-yearly; no vaccine for Paratyphi A.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ICMR 2022 STG**: azithromycin or cefixime (uncomplicated), ceftriaxone (complicated), **no empirical fluoroquinolone**; XDR (Pakistan, since 2016) - meropenem + azithromycin; **WHO 2018**: single-dose TCV from 6 months, preferred over Vi-PS.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Culture, not Widal; azithromycin for uncomplicated, ceftriaxone for complicated; typhoid ulcers longitudinal, TB ulcers transverse; marrow culture rescues the pretreated case.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected enteric fever: culture, severity, drug`,steps:[{label:`Fever over 3-5 days, step-ladder, toxic`,detail:`Relative bradycardia, leucopenia, aneosinophilia`},{label:`Blood culture 10-15 mL before antibiotic`,detail:`Marrow culture if pretreated; no single Widal`},{label:`Complicated or cannot take orally?`,detail:`Bleeding, peritonism, delirium, shock, jaundice`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No - azithromycin 1 g OD x 7 days`,detail:`Or cefixime 20 mg/kg/day x 14 days`,tone:`good`},{label:`Yes - ceftriaxone 2 g IV x 10-14 days`,detail:`Dexamethasone if delirium or shock; surgery if perforated`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Afebrile by day 3-5; else re-culture`,detail:`Abscess, cholecystitis, XDR - meropenem + azithromycin`,tone:`decision`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Diagnostic tests by week of illness`,columns:[`Week`,`Best test`,`Note`],rows:[[`1`,`Blood culture (60-80%)`,`Before first antibiotic`],[`2`,`Blood and marrow culture`,`Widal only as fourfold rise in paired sera`],[`3`,`Marrow culture; erect CXR if perforation`,`Perforation and bleeding week`],[`4+`,`Stool and urine culture`,`Detect carriers, clear food handlers`]]}]},"infectious-fever-scrub-typhus":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Scrub typhus** is caused by **Orientia tsutsugamushi**, an obligate intracellular bacterium.`,`It is spread by the bite of the **larval trombiculid mite (chigger)**.`,`Incubation is **6-21 days**, usually about 10.`,`It peaks **August to January**, after the monsoon.`,`Untreated mortality is 6-35%, falling to **under 2% with early doxycycline**.`,`Immunity is strain-specific, so **reinfection is common** and there is no vaccine.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Uncomplicated**: oral doxycycline in the OPD.`,`**Severe**: ARDS, shock, meningoencephalitis, AKI, jaundice, myocarditis or platelets under 50,000.`,`DHR-ICMR: **probable** case has an eschar, positive IgM ELISA or Weil-Felix OX-K 1:80.`,`**Confirmed** case needs IgM seroconversion, fourfold rise, IFA or PCR.`,`Scrub typhus is one of four Indian **rickettsial diseases**, all treated with doxycycline.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`The chigger feeds painlessly on **moist skin folds** under tight clothing.`,`Orientia multiplies at the bite and forms the **eschar**.`,`It spreads to regional nodes, then the bloodstream.`,`It invades endothelium and causes a **disseminated small-vessel vasculitis**.`,`Vasculitis explains pneumonitis, **ARDS**, meningoencephalitis, hepatitis, AKI and myocarditis.`,`Orientia **lacks peptidoglycan** and lives inside cells, so **beta-lactams do not work**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about abrupt fever, **severe headache, myalgia** and red eyes.`,`Ask about farm, forest or plantation work, or **sitting on grass**, 1-3 weeks earlier.`,`Ask about a painless sore or scab in the armpit, groin or under the breasts.`,`Fever persisting on **ceftriaxone or amoxicillin** is a strong clue.`,`Ask danger questions: breathlessness, confusion, seizures, low urine, jaundice.`,`Ask about **pregnancy**, which changes the drug to azithromycin.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Undress fully and search for the eschar**: axilla, groin, perineum, waistband, behind ears.`,`The eschar is a **painless black crust with a red rim**, 5-20 mm.`,`Feel for **tender nodes near the eschar** and generalised lymphadenopathy.`,`Look for conjunctival injection, a maculopapular rash and hepatosplenomegaly.`,`Record **SpO2 and respiratory rate**; pneumonitis is often silent early.`,`Check for shock, irregular pulse, neck stiffness and altered sensorium.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Do not wait for serology**; an eschar or typical picture is enough to treat.`,`CBC: normal or **raised WBC with neutrophilia** and thrombocytopenia.`,`LFT: transaminases 2-5 times, high ALP and low albumin; check sodium.`,`**IgM ELISA from day 5-7** is the confirmatory test in India.`,`**PCR (56 kDa gene) on an eschar swab** is best in week 1.`,`**Weil-Felix** has only 30-50% sensitivity; a negative excludes nothing.`,`Always run malaria and dengue tests alongside; co-infection is common.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days** is the drug of choice.`,`Child: **2.2 mg/kg twice daily**, maximum 100 mg per dose.`,`Short courses **do not stain teeth** and are safe at any age.`,`Take with water and food, upright; keep 2-3 hours from milk, iron and antacids.`,`Complete **7 days** even after the fever settles, or it relapses.`,`Expect the fever to settle **within 24-48 hours**; this is a diagnostic test.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Pregnancy or intolerance: **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days**.`,`Severe disease: **IV doxycycline plus IV azithromycin** for 7 days (INTREST trial, Vellore).`,`No IV doxycycline: use IV azithromycin, or oral doxycycline by nasogastric tube.`,`**Chloramphenicol** is third line because of aplastic anaemia.`,`**Never use beta-lactams or fluoroquinolones** for scrub typhus.`,`ARDS: oxygen, lung-protective ventilation and **conservative fluids**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **48 hours**; fever beyond 72 hours on doxycycline means rethink the diagnosis.`,`Admit for hypotension, **SpO2 under 94%**, confusion, or creatinine over 2 mg/dL.`,`Also admit for jaundice, bleeding, **platelets under 50,000** or pregnancy.`,`Give the **first dose before referral**; delay drives mortality.`,`Notify cases and clusters on **IDSP/IHIP**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Wear full sleeves with trousers tucked into socks, and **permethrin-treated clothing**.`,`Use **DEET** on exposed skin and avoid sitting on grass.`,`Bathe and change clothes after field work.`,`Weekly **doxycycline 200 mg** prophylaxis only for soldiers or expeditions.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Scrub typhus drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral BD; child 2.2 mg/kg BD`,`7 days`,`Pill oesophagitis, photosensitivity; not in pregnancy`],[`Doxycycline IV (severe)`,`100 mg IV 12-hourly`,`7 days, with azithromycin`,`Switch to oral when able`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg oral OD; child 10 mg/kg`,`5 days`,`QT prolongation; drug of choice in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin IV (severe)`,`500 mg IV OD`,`7 days, with doxycycline`,`QT prolongation`],[`Chloramphenicol`,`500 mg QID; child 50-75 mg/kg/day`,`7 days`,`Aplastic anaemia, grey baby; third line`],[`Doxycycline prophylaxis`,`200 mg oral once weekly`,`During intense exposure`,`Soldiers, expeditions only`]]},{heading:`Rickettsial diseases of India`,columns:[`Disease`,`Organism`,`Vector`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Scrub typhus`,`Orientia tsutsugamushi`,`Larval mite (chigger)`,`Eschar, nodes, ARDS`],[`Indian tick typhus`,`Rickettsia conorii`,`Dog and cattle ticks`,`Rash on palms and soles`],[`Murine typhus`,`Rickettsia typhi`,`Rat flea`,`Milder fever, rat contact`],[`Epidemic typhus`,`Rickettsia prowazekii`,`Body louse`,`Overcrowding; Brill-Zinsser relapse`]]},{heading:`Scrub typhus tests`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`When`],rows:[[`IgM ELISA`,`Recent infection; kit-specific cut-off`,`Day 5-7 onwards`],[`PCR (56 kDa gene)`,`Organism DNA`,`Week 1, eschar swab or blood`],[`IFA on paired sera`,`Gold standard serology`,`Reference laboratory`],[`Weil-Felix OX-K`,`Weak support only`,`Negative never excludes`]]}],remember:[`Post-monsoon fever, **eschar**, neutrophilia, low platelets: scrub until proved otherwise.`,`The eschar is painless: you only find it by **undressing** the patient.`,`Fever settling within **48 hours of doxycycline** confirms the diagnosis.`,`No peptidoglycan, inside cells: **ceftriaxone does nothing**.`,`Short doxycycline is safe in children; **azithromycin in pregnancy**.`,`Severe: **IV doxycycline plus azithromycin** (INTREST).`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`SSC 2026: shock in severe scrub typhus - balanced crystalloid guided by dynamic measures, then noradrenaline for MAP 65.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Scrub typhus`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute febrile illness from **Orientia tsutsugamushi** (obligate intracellular), transmitted by **larval trombiculid mite (chigger)** - vector and reservoir; incubation 6-21 days.`],[`Relevant classification`,`DHR-ICMR **suspected / probable (eschar or IgM above cut-off or Weil-Felix OX-K 1:80) / confirmed** (seroconversion, IFA, PCR); clinically **uncomplicated vs severe** (organ dysfunction).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Tsutsugamushi triangle; TN, Kerala, Karnataka, Himachal, Uttarakhand, NE, Rajasthan; **August-January**; farm, plantation, grass, picnics, army field duty.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Painless chigger bite -> dermal replication -> **eschar** -> nodes -> blood -> **endothelial invasion -> disseminated vasculitis** -> pneumonitis/ARDS, meningoencephalitis, AKI, myocarditis, hepatitis; no peptidoglycan -> **beta-lactams useless**.`],[`Key history`,`Abrupt fever, severe headache, myalgia, red eyes; cough in week 2; exposure 1-3 weeks earlier; **fever persisting on ceftriaxone**; pregnancy; danger symptoms.`],[`Key examination`,`**Undress fully - eschar** (axilla, groin, perineum, under breasts, waistband, behind ears; 10-50%), regional nodes, rash day 5-7, hepatosplenomegaly, SpO2, crackles, meningism.`],[`Investigations`,`Neutrophilia, thrombocytopenia, **transaminases 2-5x**, low albumin, low Na; **IgM ELISA from day 5-7**; **eschar PCR (56 kDa)** in week 1; Weil-Felix 30-50% sensitive; malaria and dengue alongside.`],[`Treatment - general`,`**Do not wait for serology**; OPD for uncomplicated, daily review; fluids, paracetamol; first dose before referral.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days**; child **2.2 mg/kg BD** (max 100 mg) - short courses do not stain teeth; with water and food, upright, 2-3 h from milk and iron.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Pregnancy or intolerance: **azithromycin 500 mg OD x 5 days** (child 10 mg/kg); **severe: IV doxycycline 100 mg 12-hourly + IV azithromycin 500 mg OD x 7 days (INTREST, NEJM 2025)**; chloramphenicol third line.`],[`Severe disease care`,`ARDS - oxygen, lung-protective ventilation, **conservative fluids**; shock - balanced crystalloid then **noradrenaline MAP 65**; AKI - early dialysis; HLH - specialist.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - **azithromycin** (fetal loss risk); children - doxycycline safe short course; no FQ or beta-lactams.`],[`Complications`,`**ARDS (main killer)**, meningoencephalitis (lymphocytic CSF), myocarditis, AKI, hepatitis, DIC, HLH, hyponatraemia.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`RR over 24 or SpO2 under 94%, altered sensorium, SBP under 90, **creatinine over 2**, jaundice, **platelets under 50,000**, pregnancy, unable to take oral drugs.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Defervescence within 48 h is a therapeutic test**; fever over 72 h on doxycycline - wrong diagnosis or complication; reinfection possible (strain-specific immunity).`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Full clothing, **permethrin-treated clothes, DEET**, bathe after field work, clear scrub; **no vaccine**; doxycycline **200 mg weekly** prophylaxis for intense exposure; notify on IHIP.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**INTREST (NEJM 2025, Vellore)**: severe scrub typhus - **IV doxycycline + IV azithromycin x 7 days** beat either drug alone (death, complications, persistent fever); **SSC 2026** for shock - balanced crystalloid, noradrenaline for MAP 65.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Post-monsoon fever + neutrophilia + low platelets + transaminases = scrub; ceftriaxone does nothing; negative Weil-Felix excludes nothing; severe = doxy + azithro IV.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected scrub typhus: eschar to cure`,steps:[{label:`Post-monsoon fever with grass or farm exposure`,detail:`Headache, myalgia; fever not settling on beta-lactam`},{label:`Undress and search for eschar`,detail:`Axilla, groin, perineum, waistband, under breasts`},{label:`Organ dysfunction present?`,detail:`SpO2 under 94%, confusion, SBP under 90, creatinine over 2`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No - oral doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 d`,detail:`Azithromycin 500 mg x 5 d in pregnancy`,tone:`good`},{label:`Yes - IV doxycycline + IV azithromycin`,detail:`INTREST; ARDS care, conservative fluids`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Afebrile in 48 h confirms`,detail:`IgM ELISA day 5-7 or eschar PCR for record`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Rickettsial fevers of India`,columns:[`Disease`,`Vector`,`Clue`,`Weil-Felix`],rows:[[`Scrub typhus`,`Chigger mite`,`Eschar, ARDS`,`OX-K`],[`Indian tick typhus`,`Dog/cattle ticks`,`Palm-sole rash`,`OX-2, OX-19`],[`Murine typhus`,`Rat flea`,`Mild, rat contact`,`OX-19`],[`Epidemic typhus`,`Body louse`,`Overcrowding, Brill-Zinsser`,`OX-19`]]}]},"infectious-fever-urinary-tract-infection":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**UTI** is symptomatic infection of the urinary tract with significant bacteriuria.`,`**Cystitis** is bladder infection; **acute pyelonephritis** involves the kidney.`,`**E. coli causes 70-80%** of community UTI.`,`In India **over 60% of E. coli make ESBL** and over 70% resist fluoroquinolones.`,`**Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin** still keep over 90% activity.`,`Kass criterion: **10^5 CFU/mL** of one organism in a midstream sample.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Uncomplicated**: a non-pregnant pre-menopausal woman with a normal tract.`,`**Complicated**: men, pregnancy, children, diabetes, catheter, stones, obstruction or immunosuppression.`,`IDSA 2025 defines complicated by site: **any infection beyond the bladder**.`,`**Recurrent UTI**: 2 or more in 6 months, or 3 or more in 12 months.`,`**Relapse** is the same organism within 2 weeks; **reinfection** is a new organism later.`,`**Asymptomatic bacteriuria** is treated only in **pregnancy** and before urological procedures.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Gut E. coli colonises the perineum and vaginal introitus.`,`It **ascends the short female urethra**, helped by intercourse.`,`**Type 1 fimbriae** bind the bladder; **P fimbriae** bind renal epithelium.`,`Stasis, residual urine, oestrogen loss and spermicides defeat normal defences.`,`Reflux or ascent carries organisms to the kidney, causing pyelonephritis.`,`Obstruction turns pyelonephritis into **pyonephrosis**, which needs drainage.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Dysuria and frequency **without vaginal discharge** make UTI over 90% likely.`,`**Fever, rigors and flank pain** mean pyelonephritis, which changes the drug.`,`Ask about discharge or itching; vaginitis, cervicitis and PID mimic UTI.`,`Ask **pregnancy status**: it forbids quinolones and needs a test-of-cure culture.`,`Ask about diabetes, stones, prostate symptoms, catheter and recent antibiotics.`,`In the elderly, **do not blame confusion on a positive dipstick** alone.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Screen for urosepsis with **NEWS2 or SIRS**, not qSOFA alone.`,`Feel for **suprapubic tenderness** and a palpable bladder.`,`Elicit **renal angle tenderness** for pyelonephritis.`,`In men, a **boggy, very tender prostate** on gentle DRE means acute prostatitis.`,`Cervical motion tenderness means **PID, not UTI**.`,`In children check BP, growth, spine, phimosis and a palpable bladder.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Uncomplicated cystitis needs no culture**; symptoms or dipstick are enough.`,`**Nitrite is about 95% specific**; leucocyte esterase is sensitive.`,`**Culture before the first dose** in pyelonephritis, men, pregnancy, children and recurrence.`,`Three or more organisms on culture means **contamination**; repeat the sample.`,`**Ultrasound** for men, pregnancy, diabetes, stones, or fever beyond 48-72 hours.`,`Child: **ultrasound after the first febrile UTI**; DMSA at 4-6 months for scarring.`,`Sterile pyuria: **three early-morning urines** for AFB and CBNAAT.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Cystitis: **nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days**.`,`Or **fosfomycin 3 g single dose**, at bedtime after emptying the bladder.`,`**No empirical ciprofloxacin, cefixime or cotrimoxazole** in India.`,`**Nitrofurantoin never treats pyelonephritis** or prostatitis.`,`Avoid nitrofurantoin if **eGFR under 30**, in G6PD deficiency and after 36 weeks.`,`Advise 2-3 litres of fluid, complete voiding and **voiding after intercourse**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`OPD pyelonephritis: one dose of **ceftriaxone 1-2 g or amikacin 15 mg/kg**.`,`Then an oral drug by culture: **7 days** total, or 5-7 days of a fluoroquinolone.`,`Admit for sepsis, vomiting, pregnancy, obstruction or known ESBL.`,`IV options: **piperacillin-tazobactam**, or **meropenem or ertapenem** for severe ESBL.`,`Complicated UTI that improves: **7 days**, even in men and bacteraemia.`,`Suspected prostatitis needs 10-14 days; acute bacterial prostatitis 2-4 weeks.`,`Urosepsis with obstruction needs **nephrostomy or stent the same day**.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Screen every pregnancy** with a urine culture at the first visit.`,`Pregnancy: nitrofurantoin, cephalexin, amoxicillin-clavulanate or fosfomycin for 5-7 days.`,`Avoid quinolones and tetracyclines in pregnancy; avoid cotrimoxazole in first trimester and near term.`,`**Admit pyelonephritis in pregnancy** for IV ceftriaxone.`,`Infants under 3 months or toxic children: **IV ceftriaxone 50-75 mg/kg** daily.`,`CAUTI: **replace the catheter** before culture; never treat asymptomatic catheter bacteriuria.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Uncomplicated cystitis needs **no routine repeat culture**.`,`Fever at 48-72 hours means **image for obstruction or abscess**.`,`Pregnancy: **test-of-cure culture** 1-2 weeks after treatment.`,`Every man with febrile UTI: look for obstruction, stones or prostate disease.`,`Recurrent UTI: vaginal oestrogen after menopause, or **methenamine hippurate 1 g twice daily**.`,`Long-term nitrofurantoin: check creatinine, LFT and lungs every 6 months.`]}],tables:[{heading:`UTI drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Nitrofurantoin`,`100 mg oral BD`,`5 days`,`Avoid eGFR under 30, G6PD, after 36 weeks`],[`Fosfomycin trometamol`,`3 g oral sachet`,`Single dose`,`Diarrhoea, vaginitis; ESBL active`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS`,`5-7 days`,`Diarrhoea; culture-guided; safe in pregnancy`],[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg oral BD-QID`,`5-7 days`,`Rash; safe in pregnancy`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV/IM OD; child 50-75 mg/kg`,`Stat, then oral by culture`,`Fails against ESBL`],[`Amikacin`,`15 mg/kg IV/IM OD`,`Stat or short course`,`Nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`,`7 days total if improving`,`Carbapenem-sparing for mild ESBL`],[`Meropenem or ertapenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly; ertapenem 1 g OD`,`7 days total if improving`,`Reserve for severe ESBL or urosepsis`],[`Ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin`,`500 mg BD; levo 750 mg OD`,`7 days; levo 5 days`,`Only if susceptible; tendon rupture, QT`],[`Methenamine hippurate`,`1 g oral BD`,`Long term`,`Non-antibiotic prophylaxis; not in CKD`]]},{heading:`Significant bacteriuria cut-offs`,columns:[`Sample and setting`,`Significant count (CFU/mL)`,`Note`],rows:[[`Midstream urine (Kass)`,`10^5 or more`,`Single organism`],[`Symptomatic woman, cystitis`,`10^3 or more`,`Culture not routine`],[`Acute pyelonephritis`,`10^4 or more`,`Always culture`],[`Asymptomatic bacteriuria`,`10^5 in 2 samples (women)`,`Treat only in pregnancy`],[`Suprapubic aspirate`,`Any growth`,`Gold standard in infants`]]},{heading:`Uncomplicated versus complicated`,columns:[`Feature`,`Uncomplicated`,`Complicated`],rows:[[`Who`,`Healthy non-pregnant woman, normal tract`,`Men, pregnancy, children, diabetes, catheter, obstruction`],[`Culture`,`Not needed for cystitis`,`Before the first dose`],[`Drug`,`Nitrofurantoin or fosfomycin`,`By culture; IV if unwell`],[`Duration`,`5 days or single dose`,`7 days if improving`],[`Imaging`,`None`,`Ultrasound`]]}],remember:[`Cystitis in India: **nitrofurantoin 5 days or fosfomycin 3 g once**.`,`Nitrofurantoin only reaches the bladder, **never the kidney**.`,`Treat asymptomatic bacteriuria only in **pregnancy** and before urological surgery.`,`Fever plus hydronephrosis is a **drainage problem**, not an antibiotic problem.`,`Sterile pyuria in India: **genitourinary TB** until proved otherwise.`,`Ultrasound after the **first febrile UTI** in every child.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`IDSA 2025 cUTI: complicated UTI defined by site (beyond the bladder), not sex - afebrile cystitis in men may be treated as uncomplicated.`,`IDSA 2025 cUTI: 5-7 days of a fluoroquinolone or 7 days of a non-fluoroquinolone for pyelonephritis/cUTI that is improving, instead of 10-14 days; 7 days also for Gram-negative bacteraemia; 10-14 days if prostatitis suspected.`,`SSC 2026: NEWS2 or SIRS rather than qSOFA to screen for urosepsis; balanced crystalloid and prolonged beta-lactam infusion in urosepsis.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Urinary tract infection`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Symptomatic infection with significant bacteriuria: **cystitis** (lower) or **acute pyelonephritis** (upper); Kass **10^5 CFU/mL**, 10^3 in symptomatic woman, any growth on SPA.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Uncomplicated** (non-pregnant pre-menopausal woman, normal tract) vs **complicated** (men, pregnancy, child, diabetes, catheter, stones, obstruction); **IDSA 2025: complicated = beyond the bladder**; recurrent = 2 in 6 months or 3 in 12.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**E. coli 70-80%**, Klebsiella, Proteus, Enterococcus, S. saprophyticus; India: **ESBL over 60%, FQ resistance over 70%**, nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin over 90% active.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Gut E. coli -> perineum -> short urethra -> **type 1 fimbriae** (bladder) / **P fimbriae** (kidney) -> intracellular communities (recurrence); obstruction -> **pyonephrosis needs drainage**; catheter biofilm.`],[`Key history`,`Dysuria + frequency **without discharge** (over 90% probability), fever, flank pain, vomiting, pregnancy, men - discharge/perineal pain, diabetes, catheter, recurrences, recent antibiotics.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals, **NEWS2/SIRS** for urosepsis, suprapubic tenderness, palpable bladder, **renal angle tenderness**, gentle DRE (boggy tender prostate), speculum if discharge.`],[`Investigations`,`Uncomplicated cystitis - **no culture**; dipstick nitrite specific, LE sensitive; **culture before first dose** in pyelonephritis, men, pregnancy, child, catheter, recurrence; USG if febrile at 48-72 h.`],[`Treatment - cystitis`,`**Nitrofurantoin 100 mg BD x 5 days** (not eGFR under 30, not after 36 weeks) or **fosfomycin 3 g single dose at bedtime**; **no empirical cipro, cefixime, cotrimoxazole**.`],[`Treatment - pyelonephritis (OPD)`,`**Ceftriaxone 1-2 g or amikacin 15 mg/kg stat**, then culture-guided oral: **5-7 days FQ or 7 days non-FQ** total if improving (IDSA 2025); nitrofurantoin never for kidney.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Admit: **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g 6-8-hourly** (mild ESBL) or **meropenem 1 g 8-hourly / ertapenem 1 g OD**; oral switch afebrile 48 h; **7 days total if improving**, prostatitis 2-4 weeks.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Pyonephrosis or obstructed infected stone - **same-day nephrostomy or stent**; CAUTI - change catheter before culture, treat 7 days (10-14 if slow); emphysematous - drainage.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: screen and treat ASB, nitrofurantoin/cephalexin/amox-clav/fosfomycin 5-7 days, test of cure, pyelonephritis admitted; **child**: USG after first febrile UTI, MCU if abnormal, DMSA at 4-6 months; elderly - don't treat dipstick.`],[`Complications`,`Urosepsis, pyonephrosis, perinephric abscess, **emphysematous pyelonephritis** (diabetics), papillary necrosis, renal scarring (children), preterm labour.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Urosepsis, hydronephrosis with fever, pregnancy pyelonephritis, infant under 3 months, diabetic not better at 48 h, carbapenem-resistant isolate, sterile pyuria (TB).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`No test of cure in well non-pregnant women; **pyelonephritis review at 48-72 h - persistent fever means image, not a second antibiotic**; every man with febrile UTI - look for obstruction.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Fluids 1.5 L extra/day (halves recurrence), post-coital voiding, stop spermicides; **vaginal oestrogen** post-menopause; **methenamine hippurate 1 g BD** (ALTAR); nitrofurantoin 50-100 mg nightly x 6-12 months.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IDSA 2025**: site-based cUTI definition, **7-day courses** for improving cUTI and bacteraemia; **SSC 2026**: NEWS2/SIRS not qSOFA; **EAU 2026** limited update - non-antibiotic cystitis options, fungal UTI chapter.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Treat ASB only in pregnancy and before mucosa-breaching urology; fever + hydronephrosis = drainage; sterile pyuria in India = GU TB; confused elderly + positive dipstick usually ASB.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Dysuria in the OPD: which UTI and what to do`,steps:[{label:`Dysuria/frequency without discharge`,detail:`Vaginal discharge - think vaginitis, PID, urethritis`},{label:`Fever, flank pain or vomiting?`,detail:`Beyond the bladder = complicated (IDSA 2025)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No - cystitis: no culture if uncomplicated`,detail:`Nitrofurantoin 100 mg BD x 5 d or fosfomycin 3 g once`,tone:`good`},{label:`Yes - culture, ceftriaxone or amikacin stat`,detail:`Oral by culture: 5-7 d FQ or 7 d other`},{label:`Sepsis or obstruction?`,detail:`NEWS2/SIRS, USG; hydronephrosis = drain same day`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Still febrile at 48-72 h - image`,detail:`Abscess, emphysematous pyelonephritis, stone`,tone:`decision`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`UTI antibiotic ladder for India (ESBL era)`,steps:[{label:`Cystitis`,detail:`Nitrofurantoin 100 mg BD x 5 d; fosfomycin 3 g single dose`,tone:`good`},{label:`Mild pyelonephritis`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 1-2 g or amikacin 15 mg/kg stat; 7 days total`},{label:`Severe or mild ESBL`,detail:`Piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`},{label:`Urosepsis or severe ESBL`,detail:`Meropenem 1 g 8-hourly or ertapenem 1 g OD; drain`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Duration if improving`,detail:`7 days cUTI (incl. men, bacteraemia); 10-14 if prostatitis`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Special groups in UTI`,columns:[`Group`,`Key rule`,`Drug`],rows:[[`Pregnancy`,`Screen and treat ASB; test of cure`,`Nitrofurantoin (not after 36 wk), cephalexin, fosfomycin`],[`Child`,`USG after first febrile UTI`,`Ceftriaxone 50-75 mg/kg or oral cefixime 8 mg/kg/day`],[`Elderly`,`Do not treat a dipstick`,`Culture only with symptoms or fever`],[`Catheter`,`Change catheter before culture`,`7 days if prompt response`]]}]},"infectious-fever-skin-soft-tissue-infection":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**SSTI** is infection of the epidermis, dermis, fat, fascia or muscle.`,`**Purulent infection is Staphylococcus aureus**; non-purulent cellulitis is **Streptococcus pyogenes**.`,`About **40% of Indian S. aureus is MRSA**.`,`Community MRSA with **PVL toxin** causes recurrent boils and household clusters.`,`Almost every SSTI starts with a **barrier breach** such as tinea pedis, a cut or a bite.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`By depth: impetigo and folliculitis, then **erysipelas and cellulitis**, then abscess, then necrotising infection.`,`IDSA: **purulent versus non-purulent**, each graded mild, moderate or severe.`,`**Moderate** means systemic signs: temperature over 38 C, HR over 90, RR over 24.`,`**Severe** means hypotension, failed oral therapy, immunocompromise, bullae or rapid spread.`,`Necrotising fasciitis: **type I polymicrobial**, **type II streptococcal**, type III clostridial or Vibrio.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Streptococcal **hyaluronidase and streptokinase** spread infection diffusely, without pus.`,`**Erysipelas** is upper dermis with lymphatics, so it is raised with a **sharp margin**.`,`Cellulitis is deeper, so its **margin is indistinct**.`,`Staphylococcal **coagulase** walls infection off, forming a localised abscess.`,`Pus is avascular, so antibiotics penetrate poorly; **drainage cures the abscess**.`,`In necrotising fasciitis, organisms race along the fascia and **thrombose perforating vessels**.`,`Deep ischaemia causes **pain out of proportion** before the skin changes.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **how fast it spreads** and how painful it is.`,`Ask about **pus or a boil**; purulence means S. aureus and drainage.`,`Ask about fever, vomiting or confusion, which raise the severity grade.`,`Ask the portal: injury, insect bite, **itchy toe webs**, scabies, injection.`,`Ask about bites, water exposure, diabetes and immunosuppression.`,`Ask about **tetanus vaccination** and penicillin allergy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Mark the edge of erythema with a pen**, with date and time.`,`Feel for **fluctuation**; use bedside ultrasound if unsure.`,`Necrotising signs: **tense oedema beyond the redness, dusky skin, bullae, anaesthesia, crepitus**.`,`Check the **toe webs for tinea pedis** and look for lymphoedema.`,`Exclude mimics: DVT, venous stasis dermatitis, gout; **bilateral cellulitis is rare**.`,`Diabetic foot: **probe-to-bone test**, pulses, ABI and monofilament.`,`Around the eye, proptosis or painful eye movement means **orbital cellulitis**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Diagnosis is clinical**; mild cellulitis needs no tests.`,`Swabs of intact cellulitic skin are useless.`,`**Culture pus** from every drained abscess.`,`Check **glucose or HbA1c** in recurrent boils and every foot infection.`,`**LRINEC of 8 or more** means high risk, but a low score never excludes necrotising fasciitis.`,`**Imaging must never delay surgery** in suspected necrotising infection.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Abscess: **incision and drainage** is the treatment; culture the pus.`,`Limited impetigo: **mupirocin or fusidic acid 2%** three times daily for 5 days.`,`Mild cellulitis: **amoxicillin 500 mg TDS or cephalexin 500 mg QID for 5 days**.`,`Penicillin allergy: **clindamycin 300-450 mg** three to four times daily.`,`Elevate the limb and treat the portal, such as tinea pedis.`,`**Never squeeze a boil** in the nose and upper-lip danger area.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Purulent infection needing antibiotic: **MRSA-active cotrimoxazole or doxycycline** for 5-7 days.`,`Moderate cellulitis: IV **cefazolin, ceftriaxone or cloxacillin**.`,`Severe or MRSA sepsis: **vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg IV** or linezolid 600 mg twice daily.`,`Necrotising fasciitis: **piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem plus clindamycin plus vancomycin**.`,`**Immediate surgical debridement** is the only thing that improves survival.`,`Clindamycin is added to **switch off toxin production**.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Bites: **amoxicillin-clavulanate**, because cloxacillin and cephalexin miss Pasteurella.`,`Bite prophylaxis lasts 3-5 days; infected bites need 7-14 days.`,`Diabetic foot soft tissue: **1-2 weeks**; osteomyelitis without resection: **6 weeks**.`,`Diabetic foot also needs off-loading, debridement, glucose control and vascular review.`,`Recurrent boils: **nasal mupirocin and chlorhexidine wash** for the whole household.`,`Recurrent cellulitis: **penicillin V 250 mg twice daily for 12 months**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review cellulitis at **48-72 hours** against the pen mark.`,`Spread beyond 24 hours means failure: look for abscess, necrosis or MRSA.`,`Refer the same day for necrotising infection, orbital cellulitis or flexor tenosynovitis.`,`Tetanus-prone wound with under 3 doses: **Td plus tetanus immunoglobulin 250 IU**.`,`After impetigo, check for oedema and haematuria at 2-3 weeks for **post-streptococcal GN**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`SSTI drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Mupirocin or fusidic acid 2%`,`Topical TDS`,`5 days, never over 10`,`Resistance with long use`],[`Amoxicillin`,`500 mg oral TDS`,`5 days`,`Rash; non-purulent cellulitis`],[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg oral QID`,`5-7 days`,`GI upset, rash`],[`Cloxacillin`,`500 mg oral QID; IV 1-2 g 6-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Empty stomach; flucloxacillin cholestasis`],[`Clindamycin`,`300-450 mg oral TDS-QID; 600-900 mg IV`,`5-7 days`,`C. difficile diarrhoea`],[`Cotrimoxazole DS`,`1-2 tablets oral BD`,`5-7 days`,`Rash, SJS, hyperkalaemia; MRSA cover`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral BD`,`5-7 days`,`Photosensitivity; not in pregnancy`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS`,`Bites 3-5 days; infected 7-14`,`Diarrhoea, cholestatic hepatitis`],[`Vancomycin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly`,`Until oral switch`,`Nephrotoxicity, red man reaction`],[`Linezolid`,`600 mg oral or IV BD`,`Short courses`,`Low platelets after 2 weeks; avoid with SSRIs`]]},{heading:`Purulent versus non-purulent`,columns:[`Feature`,`Purulent`,`Non-purulent`],rows:[[`Organism`,`Staphylococcus aureus`,`Streptococcus pyogenes`],[`Lesions`,`Furuncle, carbuncle, abscess`,`Cellulitis, erysipelas`],[`Mild`,`Incision and drainage`,`Oral amoxicillin or cephalexin, 5 days`],[`Moderate`,`Drainage plus cotrimoxazole or doxycycline`,`IV penicillin, ceftriaxone or cefazolin`],[`Severe`,`Drainage plus vancomycin or linezolid`,`Explore surgically; broad IV cover`]]},{heading:`LRINEC score`,columns:[`Component`,`Finding`,`Points`],rows:[[`CRP`,`150 mg/L or more`,`4`],[`WBC`,`15,000-25,000 (over 25,000 scores 2)`,`1`],[`Haemoglobin`,`11-13.5 g/dL (under 11 scores 2)`,`1`],[`Sodium`,`Under 135 mmol/L`,`2`],[`Creatinine`,`Over 1.6 mg/dL`,`2`],[`Glucose`,`Over 180 mg/dL`,`1`]]}],remember:[`**Pus = staph, no pus = strep**: this one rule picks the antibiotic.`,`An abscess is cured by a **scalpel**; the antibiotic is the adjunct.`,`Five days of amoxicillin or cephalexin is enough for simple cellulitis.`,`**Pain out of proportion** means necrotising fasciitis: surgeon tonight.`,`Probe every diabetic foot ulcer: touching bone means **osteomyelitis**.`,`Skin strep causes glomerulonephritis but **not rheumatic fever**.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`SSC 2026: sepsis from cellulitis - at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid and prolonged beta-lactam infusion after the loading dose.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Skin and soft tissue infection`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Microbial invasion of epidermis to muscle - impetigo, folliculitis, erysipelas, cellulitis, abscess, **necrotising fasciitis**; classified by depth and **pus**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IDSA purulent vs non-purulent, mild/moderate/severe** (moderate = SIRS signs; severe = hypotension, failed oral, immunocompromise, bullae); NSTI **type I polymicrobial, II GAS/S. aureus, III clostridial/Vibrio**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Purulent = S. aureus (about 40% MRSA in India)**, non-purulent = **S. pyogenes**; tinea pedis, lymphoedema (filarial), venous disease, diabetes, obesity; bites - Pasteurella, Eikenella.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Barrier breach -> strep hyaluronidase spreads (diffuse cellulitis) / staph coagulase walls off (abscess); NSTI spreads along fascia -> perforator thrombosis -> **pain out of proportion -> anaesthetic, dusky, crepitant skin**.`],[`Key history`,`Speed of spread, **pain out of proportion**, pus, systemic symptoms, portal (toe webs, bites, injection), diabetes, previous episodes, recurrent boils in household, tetanus status, allergy.`],[`Key examination`,`**Mark the margin with date and time**, fluctuation (bedside USG), lymphangitis, necrotising signs (oedema beyond erythema, bullae, crepitus), toe webs, Kanavel signs, probe-to-bone and pulses in diabetic foot.`],[`Investigations`,`Clinical diagnosis; **culture pus** after drainage; blood cultures in moderate/severe; glucose/HbA1c in recurrent boils; **LRINEC 6-7 intermediate, 8+ high - low score never excludes**; imaging never delays surgery.`],[`Treatment - general`,`**Incision and drainage** is the cure for abscess; elevate limb; treat tinea pedis and lymphoedema; never squeeze facial danger-area boils; tetanus Td/TIG.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Non-purulent mild: **amoxicillin 500 mg TDS or cephalexin 500 mg QID x 5 days**; impetigo limited - **mupirocin 2% TDS x 5 days**; extensive - cephalexin or cloxacillin 5-7 days; allergy - clindamycin 300-450 mg TDS.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Purulent needing antibiotic: **cotrimoxazole DS 1-2 BD or doxycycline 100 mg BD x 5-7 days**; moderate: IV cefazolin 1-2 g 8-hourly or ceftriaxone; MRSA sepsis: **vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg 8-12-hourly** or linezolid 600 mg BD.`],[`Necrotising fasciitis`,`**Immediate debridement** (re-look 24-48 h) + **piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem + clindamycin 600-900 mg 8-hourly + vancomycin/linezolid**; GAS - penicillin + clindamycin; mortality 20-30%.`],[`Special groups`,`Bites: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 3-5 days prophylaxis** (hand, face, cat, human), 7-14 if infected; diabetic foot (IWGDF/IDSA 2023): **1-2 weeks for soft tissue** (longer if extensive or severe PAD), osteomyelitis 6 weeks (3 after minor amputation with positive margin).`],[`Complications`,`NSTI, streptococcal toxic shock, orbital cellulitis, cavernous sinus thrombosis, flexor tenosynovitis, osteomyelitis, **post-streptococcal GN** (not rheumatic fever).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Pain out of proportion, bullae, crepitus, rapid spread, sepsis, proptosis, danger-area boil with headache, Kanavel signs, Fournier gangrene, diabetic foot with systemic signs.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review at **48-72 h against the pen mark**; slight spread in 24 h is expected; oral switch afebrile 48 h; recurrent boils - glucose, HIV, **mupirocin nasal + chlorhexidine 5-7 days** for household.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Recurrent cellulitis: **penicillin V 250 mg BD x 12 months (PATCH I)** or benzathine penicillin; filarial MMDP limb hygiene; compression once settled.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IWGDF/IDSA 2023** diabetic foot: 1-2 weeks for soft-tissue infection of any severity, 6 weeks osteomyelitis (3 weeks after minor amputation with positive bone margin); **SSC 2026** cellulitis sepsis: 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid, prolonged beta-lactam infusion.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Purulent = staph = drain; non-purulent = strep = 5 days; clindamycin switches off toxin (Eagle effect); probe to bone = osteomyelitis; never suture bites.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Red, hot limb: decide in one look`,steps:[{label:`Pain out of proportion, bullae, crepitus?`,detail:`Oedema beyond erythema, anaesthetic dusky skin`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - necrotising fasciitis`,detail:`Fluids, cultures, pip-taz + clindamycin; surgeon tonight`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Pus or fluctuation?`,detail:`Bedside USG if unsure`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Purulent - incision and drainage`,detail:`Culture; cotrimoxazole or doxycycline if risk features`},{label:`Non-purulent cellulitis`,detail:`Amoxicillin or cephalexin x 5 days; mark margin, elevate`},{label:`Review 48-72 h against pen mark`,detail:`Treat tinea pedis and lymphoedema`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`SSTI antibiotic ladder`,steps:[{label:`Limited impetigo`,detail:`Mupirocin or fusidic acid 2% TDS x 5 days`},{label:`Mild cellulitis`,detail:`Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS or cephalexin 500 mg QID x 5 days`},{label:`Purulent with risk / MRSA`,detail:`Cotrimoxazole DS 1-2 BD or doxycycline 100 mg BD x 5-7 d`},{label:`Moderate - IV`,detail:`Cefazolin 1-2 g 8-hourly or ceftriaxone 1-2 g OD`},{label:`Severe / NSTI`,detail:`Pip-taz or meropenem + clindamycin + vancomycin; debride`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"infectious-fever-rabies-animal-bite":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Rabies** is an almost always fatal encephalomyelitis caused by a **Lyssavirus** (Rhabdoviridae).`,`It spreads in the saliva of a rabid animal; **dogs cause about 96%** of Indian cases.`,`India has about **36% of world rabies deaths**; 40% of victims are children.`,`Rodents, squirrels, rabbits and birds need **no PEP** in India.`,`Rabies is **completely preventable** with wound washing, vaccine and immunoglobulin.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Category I**: touching, feeding, licks on intact skin; no PEP.`,`**Category II**: nibbling, minor scratches **without bleeding**; wash and vaccinate.`,`**Category III**: bites, scratches with blood, licks on broken skin, mucosa, **any bat contact**.`,`Category III needs wash, vaccine **and RIG or monoclonal antibody**.`,`Category II in an **immunocompromised** person is managed as category III.`,`Clinical forms: **furious (80%)** with hydrophobia, or **paralytic (20%)**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Virus first replicates in **muscle at the wound**; washing and RIG act here.`,`It binds the **acetylcholine receptor** and enters peripheral nerves, beyond antibody reach.`,`It travels to the brain by **retrograde axonal transport**.`,`Incubation is usually **20-90 days**, shorter for face, hand and multiple bites.`,`Brainstem and limbic infection cause **hydrophobia, aerophobia** and agitation.`,`**Negri bodies** are found in hippocampal and Purkinje cells.`,`A rabid dog or cat dies within about **10 days** of shedding virus.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **which animal** and exactly what happened: lick, scratch or bite.`,`Ask **did it bleed**; blood makes it category III.`,`Ask site and number of wounds; face, hands and genitals carry most risk.`,`Ask about **previous complete rabies vaccination**; it changes the regimen.`,`Ask about immunosuppression, chloroquine or steroids, which blunt vaccine response.`,`Pregnancy and breastfeeding are **never contraindications**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Undress and examine every wound**; count, measure and grade each one.`,`Grade the category from the **worst wound** and record it on the card.`,`Check tendon, nerve, joint and vessel injury in hand and face wounds.`,`Record **weight** for the RIG dose; the vaccine dose is not weight-based.`,`In clinical rabies, look for **hydrophobia, aerophobia** and hypersalivation.`,`Use **barrier precautions**; saliva and tears contain virus.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No test is needed to start PEP**; the decision is clinical.`,`Do not check routine antibody titres in healthy people.`,`Check **RFFIT titre** in the immunocompromised; **0.5 IU/mL or more** is protective.`,`If the animal dies, send its brain for **dFAT** testing.`,`Human diagnosis: **nuchal skin biopsy**, saliva RT-PCR, corneal smear.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Wash every wound with soap and running water for 15 minutes**.`,`Then apply **povidone iodine or 70% alcohol**; never chilli, turmeric or oil.`,`**Do not suture**; if unavoidable, infiltrate RIG first and suture loosely later.`,`**Start vaccine on day 0**, however late the patient presents.`,`IM dose is **one full vial at any age**, into deltoid or thigh, **never gluteal**.`,`Category III: infiltrate **RIG or monoclonal antibody into every wound**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**ERIG 40 IU/kg** (max 3000 IU) or **HRIG 20 IU/kg** (max 1500 IU).`,`Monoclonal: **Rabishield 3.33 IU/kg** or **Twinrab 40 IU/kg**.`,`Give RIG once, **up to day 7** after the first vaccine dose, never later.`,`No skin test before ERIG; keep **adrenaline** ready and observe 30-60 minutes.`,`Dilute RIG with saline if the volume is too small for many wounds.`,`No RIG available: start vaccine and **refer the same day**.`,`Bite prophylaxis: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 3-5 days**; Td if no dose in 5 years.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Previously fully vaccinated: **two doses on days 0 and 3**, and no RIG.`,`Immunocompromised: RIG for category II and III, full course, then titre check.`,`**Pre-exposure prophylaxis** for vets, animal handlers, lab staff and high-risk children.`,`Clinical rabies is virtually always fatal: **palliative care** in a quiet, dim room.`,`Staff or family with saliva exposure to broken skin need PEP.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Observe the dog for **10 days**, but never delay PEP for observation.`,`A **missed dose is resumed**, not restarted.`,`Warn ERIG recipients about **serum sickness at 7-14 days**.`,`Refer severe, multiple or facial bites and any suspected clinical rabies.`,`Report bites on **IDSP/IHIP**; NAPRE aims at **70% dog vaccination coverage**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Rabies PEP drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cell culture vaccine (IM)`,`1 full vial IM, deltoid or thigh`,`Essen or Zagreb schedule`,`Local pain, fever; never gluteal`],[`Cell culture vaccine (ID)`,`0.1 mL at 2 sites`,`Days 0, 3, 7, 28`,`Must raise a bleb; vial within 6-8 h`],[`Equine RIG`,`40 IU/kg, max 3000 IU, into wounds`,`Once, day 0 to day 7`,`Anaphylaxis rare; serum sickness`],[`Human RIG`,`20 IU/kg, max 1500 IU, into wounds`,`Once, day 0 to day 7`,`Never same site as vaccine`],[`Monoclonal antibody`,`Rabishield 3.33 IU/kg; Twinrab 40 IU/kg`,`Once, day 0 to day 7`,`Not for previously vaccinated`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS`,`3-5 days; 7-14 if infected`,`Diarrhoea, rash`],[`Td with or without TIG`,`Td 0.5 mL IM; TIG 250 IU IM`,`Single dose`,`TIG if under 3 doses or unknown`]]},{heading:`WHO exposure categories`,columns:[`Category`,`Contact`,`Action`],rows:[[`I`,`Touching, feeding, licks on intact skin`,`Wash skin; no PEP`],[`II`,`Nibbling, scratches without bleeding`,`Wash and vaccinate`],[`III`,`Bites, bleeding scratches, mucosa, bats`,`Wash, vaccinate and RIG`]]},{heading:`Vaccine schedules`,columns:[`Regimen`,`Dose`,`Days`],rows:[[`Essen IM`,`1 vial`,`0, 3, 7, 14, 28`],[`Zagreb IM (2-1-1)`,`1 vial per site`,`Two on day 0, one each on days 7 and 21`],[`Thai Red Cross ID`,`0.1 mL at 2 sites`,`0, 3, 7, 28`],[`Re-exposure after full course`,`1 vial IM or 0.1 mL ID`,`0 and 3; no RIG`],[`Pre-exposure (NRCP)`,`1 vial IM or 0.1 mL ID`,`0, 7, 21 or 28`]]}],remember:[`**15 minutes of soap and running water** halves the rabies risk.`,`**Blood at the wound = category III**: vaccine plus RIG.`,`One full vial IM at any age; **never the buttock**.`,`Essen **0-3-7-14-28**; Zagreb **2-1-1**; ID 2 sites on 0-3-7-28.`,`Previously vaccinated: days 0 and 3, **no RIG**.`,`**No time limit, no contraindication**; a missed dose is resumed, not restarted.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Animal bite and rabies PEP`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Almost always fatal **Lyssavirus (Rhabdoviridae) encephalomyelitis** from infected saliva; **dogs cause 96-97%** of Indian cases; India has about 36% of world rabies deaths.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**WHO category I** (touch, lick on intact skin) - none; **II** (nibble, minor scratch without bleeding) - wash + vaccine; **III** (transdermal bite, bleeding, lick on broken skin, mucosa, bat) - wash + vaccine + **RIG**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Dog, cat, monkey, mongoose, jackal; children under 15 about 40%; **rodents, squirrels, rabbits, birds need no PEP**; immunocompromised category II treated as III.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Saliva -> muscle replication at wound (window for washing and RIG) -> **nAChR binding** -> retrograde axonal spread 50-100 mm/day -> brain (limbic, brainstem, **Negri bodies**) -> centrifugal to salivary glands; incubation 20-90 days.`],[`Key history`,`Animal, what happened (**did it bleed**), when, site (face, hand = short incubation), animal's fate, past cell-culture vaccination, wound applications, immunosuppression, tetanus status.`],[`Key examination`,`Undress, count and grade each wound, deep structure injury, infection; **weight for RIG dose**; clinical rabies - hydrophobia, aerophobia, hypersalivation, ascending flaccid paralysis.`],[`Investigations`,`**None needed to start PEP**; RFFIT titre (0.5 IU/mL protective) only in immunocompromised; animal brain dFAT; human ante-mortem - nuchal biopsy, saliva RT-PCR, corneal smear.`],[`Treatment - wound care`,`**Soap and running water 15 minutes**, then povidone iodine or alcohol; **no suturing** (if unavoidable, RIG first and loose sutures); no chilli, turmeric, cautery.`],[`Treatment - vaccine`,`Cell-culture vaccine, **full vial IM at any age**, deltoid or anterolateral thigh, **never gluteal**: **Essen 0-3-7-14-28**, **Zagreb 2-1-1 (0, 7, 21)**, **ID 0.1 mL x 2 sites days 0-3-7-28** (raise a bleb).`],[`Passive immunisation`,`**ERIG 40 IU/kg (max 3000), HRIG 20 IU/kg (max 1500)**, Rabishield 3.33 IU/kg, Twinrab 40 IU/kg - **infiltrate into every wound**, once, day 0 to 7; no skin test; never in previously vaccinated.`],[`Re-exposure and PrEP`,`After complete course: **2 doses days 0 and 3, no RIG** (any interval); PrEP days 0, 7, 21/28 (NRCP) or 0 and 7 (WHO).`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy, infancy, lactation - no contraindication**; immunocompromised - RIG for category II and III, full course, titre at 2-4 weeks; missed dose - **resume, do not restart**.`],[`Adjuncts`,`Amoxicillin-clavulanate 3-5 days for deep, hand, face, cat bites; **Td 0.5 mL** if no dose in 5 years, TIG 250 IU if under 3 doses.`],[`Clinical rabies`,`Virtually 100% fatal; **palliative care** in quiet dim room, sedation, analgesia, barrier precautions; Milwaukee protocol not recommended; staff exposed get PEP.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Category III face/hand/genital or multiple bites, no RIG available (refer same day, within 7 days), wild animal or bat, immunocompromised, tingling at healed bite site.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Written dose card, trace defaulters; observe dog/cat 10 days (healthy - course may be modified) but **never delay PEP**; serum sickness watch 7-14 days after ERIG.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NAPRE 2021 - dog-mediated rabies elimination by 2030**: 70% dog vaccination, animal birth control, PEP in every PHC; notify bites on IHIP.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2018** (adopted in **NRCP 2019**): abridged regimens (ID 0-3-7-28, 1-week ID, IM 4-dose), PrEP days 0 and 7, **RIG into wounds only, no skin test**; **rabies monoclonal antibodies** (Rabishield, Twinrab) licensed in India; **NAPRE 2021** - zero dog-mediated deaths by 2030.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Blood at the wound = category III; whole vial whatever the age; RIG into the wound not the buttock; no time limit and no contraindication; resume, never restart.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Animal bite: category to complete PEP`,steps:[{label:`Wash 15 min with soap and running water`,detail:`Then povidone iodine; do not suture`},{label:`Which WHO category?`,detail:`Bleeding, transdermal, mucosa, bat = III`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Category I - reassure`,detail:`Licks on intact skin; no vaccine`,tone:`good`},{label:`Category II - vaccine`,detail:`Essen, Zagreb or ID 2-2-2-0-2; RIG if immunocompromised`},{label:`Category III - vaccine + RIG`,detail:`ERIG 40 or HRIG 20 IU/kg into every wound, day 0-7`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Previously fully vaccinated?`,detail:`Days 0 and 3 only, no RIG`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Complete schedule; Td; antibiotic if deep`,detail:`Card, reminders, notify on IHIP`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Rabies vaccine regimens at a glance`,columns:[`Regimen`,`Dose`,`Days`],rows:[[`Essen IM`,`1 vial`,`0, 3, 7, 14, 28`],[`Zagreb IM`,`1 vial per site`,`0 (x2), 7, 21`],[`Updated TRC ID`,`0.1 mL at 2 sites`,`0, 3, 7, 28`],[`Re-exposure`,`1 vial IM or 0.1 mL ID`,`0, 3 - no RIG`],[`PrEP (NRCP)`,`1 vial IM or 0.1 mL ID`,`0, 7, 21 or 28`]]}]},"infectious-fever-hiv-art":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**HIV** is a chronic retroviral infection that progressively destroys **CD4 T cells**.`,`**AIDS** is a WHO stage 4 condition or **CD4 below 200 cells/mm3**.`,`India has about **2.5 million** people living with HIV; adult prevalence about 0.2%.`,`Sexual spread is commonest; needlestick risk is about **0.3%** per exposure.`,`**TB is the commonest opportunistic infection** and killer in Indian PLHIV.`,`**Undetectable equals untransmittable (U=U)** for sexual transmission.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**WHO clinical stages 1-4** work without a laboratory; patients never move back down.`,`**Advanced HIV disease**: CD4 under 200 or stage 3-4, and **every child under 5**.`,`Advanced disease triggers CrAg screen, TB-LAM, cotrimoxazole, TB preventive therapy and rapid ART.`,`CD4 under 200: Pneumocystis; under 100: toxoplasma and cryptococcus; under 50: CMV.`,`Window period: about **2-4 weeks** for fourth-generation tests, 10-14 days for NAT.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**gp120 binds CD4** and the CCR5 or CXCR4 co-receptor; gp41 fuses the membranes.`,`Error-prone **reverse transcriptase** makes DNA, so resistance arises fast and three drugs are needed.`,`**Integrase** inserts viral DNA into the host genome; dolutegravir blocks this step.`,`Acute infection causes a **mononucleosis-like seroconversion illness** at peak infectivity.`,`Untreated, CD4 falls about **50-100 cells a year**; AIDS follows in 8-10 years.`,`A **latent reservoir** in resting memory cells makes ART **lifelong**.`,`**IRIS** is paradoxical worsening as recovering immunity reacts to TB or cryptococcus.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask risk exposures **non-judgementally**, including the spouse's HIV status.`,`Ask about weight loss, **fever or diarrhoea over 1 month**, thrush and shingles.`,`Ask the **four-symptom TB screen** at every visit: cough, fever, weight loss, night sweats.`,`Ask about headache and confusion (meningitis) and **visual floaters** (CMV retinitis).`,`Take a full drug history: **rifampicin, antacids, iron, anticonvulsants, metformin** interact.`,`Ask about adherence; **over 95%** adherence decides success.`,`Ask about hepatitis B and renal disease, which change the regimen.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **weight** every visit; loss over 10% is stage 3.`,`Look for **oral candida** and **oral hairy leukoplakia** on the lateral tongue.`,`Feel for generalised lymphadenopathy; a matted node suggests TB or lymphoma.`,`Check SpO2 at rest and after walking; **PCP desaturates on exertion**.`,`Dilated **fundoscopy** if CD4 is under 50 or vision changes.`,`Children: **parotid enlargement**, linear gingival erythema and clubbing are paediatric staging signs.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Diagnose by **NACO three-test strategy**: all three reactive means positive.`,`Testing needs **informed consent** and counselling under the HIV/AIDS Act 2017.`,`Under **18 months**, use **DNA-PCR** at 6 weeks, 6 months and 12 months.`,`Baseline: **CD4**, CBC, creatinine, LFT, glucose, HBsAg, anti-HCV, VDRL, chest X-ray.`,`CD4 under 200: **serum CrAg** and **urine TB-LAM**.`,`**Viral load at 6 and 12 months**, then yearly, is the monitoring test.`,`**Failure**: viral load 1000 or more on two tests **3 months apart**, after adherence counselling.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Treat all**, regardless of CD4, stage, age or pregnancy.`,`Start the **same day or within 7 days** of diagnosis.`,`Adults: **TLD - tenofovir 300 + lamivudine 300 + dolutegravir 50 mg**, once daily.`,`Take TLD 2 hours before or 6 hours after **antacids, iron or calcium**.`,`Avoid tenofovir if **CrCl under 50**; use zidovudine or abacavir.`,`Hepatitis B co-infection: **never stop tenofovir and lamivudine** abruptly.`,`Children from 4 weeks and 3 kg: **abacavir + lamivudine + dolutegravir** by weight band.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**With rifampicin**, add an extra **dolutegravir 50 mg 12 hours later**.`,`Continue the extra dose for **2 weeks after rifampicin** stops.`,`With a protease inhibitor, replace rifampicin with **rifabutin**.`,`TB: start ATT, then **ART within 2 weeks**; TB meningitis: defer 4-8 weeks.`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis: defer ART 4-6 weeks** after antifungals start.`,`Second line after confirmed failure: **boosted protease inhibitor** with zidovudine and lamivudine.`,`Cap metformin at **1000 mg a day** with dolutegravir.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**Cotrimoxazole DS daily** for CD4 under 350, stage 3-4, or any HIV-TB.`,`Stop cotrimoxazole when CD4 is **over 350 twice**, 6 months apart, on ART.`,`**TB preventive therapy**: isoniazid 300 mg with pyridoxine for 6 months, or 3HP.`,`**PEP: TLD for 28 days**, ideally within 2 hours, never after 72 hours.`,`PEP follow-up HIV tests at **6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months**.`,`**PrEP**: daily tenofovir plus emtricitabine cuts infection by **over 90%**.`,`After starting ART, review at **2 and 4 weeks**, then monthly until stable.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Every HIV-positive pregnant woman starts **lifelong TLD immediately**.`,`Viral load **under 1000 at 36 weeks: vaginal delivery**; otherwise caesarean at 38 weeks.`,`Low-risk infant: **nevirapine syrup daily for 6 weeks**.`,`High-risk infant: nevirapine 12 weeks plus zidovudine 6 weeks.`,`**Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months** with mother on ART; never mixed feeding.`,`A **positive infant DNA-PCR** means same-day ART.`]}],tables:[{heading:`HIV drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`TLD (TDF + 3TC + DTG)`,`300/300/50 mg oral OD`,`Lifelong; PEP 28 days`,`Weight gain, insomnia; TDF kidney and bone`],[`TLE (with efavirenz)`,`Efavirenz 400 mg at bedtime`,`Lifelong, if DTG unsuitable`,`Vivid dreams, depression, rash`],[`Abacavir + lamivudine`,`600/300 mg OD; child by weight band`,`Lifelong`,`Hypersensitivity; never rechallenge`],[`Zidovudine`,`300 mg oral BD`,`Lifelong`,`Anaemia; avoid if Hb under 8`],[`Atazanavir/r or lopinavir/r`,`300/100 mg OD; 400/100 mg BD`,`Second line`,`Jaundice (ATV), diarrhoea (LPV); no rifampicin`],[`Nevirapine (infant)`,`10 mg OD (2000-2499 g); 15 mg (2500 g+)`,`6 weeks; 12 if high risk`,`Rash, hepatitis in adults`],[`Cotrimoxazole DS`,`800/160 mg oral OD`,`Until CD4 over 350 twice`,`Rash, SJS, cytopenias`],[`Isoniazid (TPT)`,`300 mg OD with pyridoxine`,`6 months`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy`],[`Fluconazole (CrAg positive)`,`800 mg, then 400 mg, then 200 mg`,`2 weeks, 8 weeks, then maintenance`,`Hepatitis, QT prolongation`],[`Tenofovir + emtricitabine (PrEP)`,`One tablet oral daily`,`While at risk`,`HIV test every 3 months`]]},{heading:`WHO clinical stages (adults)`,columns:[`Stage`,`Examples`],rows:[[`1`,`Asymptomatic; persistent generalised lymphadenopathy`],[`2`,`Weight loss under 10%, zoster, recurrent URTI, oral ulcers`],[`3`,`Weight loss over 10%, oral candida, hairy leukoplakia, pulmonary TB`],[`4`,`PCP, oesophageal candida, extrapulmonary TB, cryptococcus, toxoplasma, Kaposi`]]},{heading:`Exposure and PEP action`,columns:[`Exposure`,`Risk`,`Action`],rows:[[`Deep needlestick with visible blood`,`About 0.3%`,`TLD 28 days, within 2-72 hours`],[`Mucosal splash or superficial scratch`,`About 0.09%`,`TLD 28 days; stop if source negative`],[`Sexual assault`,`0.1% vaginal, 1.4% anal per act`,`TLD, emergency contraception, STI cover`],[`Partner with sustained suppression`,`Effectively zero`,`No PEP`],[`Intact skin, fluids without blood`,`None`,`Wash and reassure`],[`After 72 hours`,`PEP ineffective`,`Test and consider PrEP`]]}],remember:[`**Treat all, same day**: TLD, one tablet once daily.`,`Wait only for **cryptococcal (4-6 weeks)** and **TB meningitis (4-8 weeks)**.`,`Rifampicin: **extra dolutegravir 50 mg** 12 hours later.`,`One high viral load is **non-adherence** until proved otherwise.`,`PEP: TLD **28 days**, ideally 2 hours, never after 72.`,`Never stop tenofovir and lamivudine abruptly in **hepatitis B**.`,`Under 18 months, **DNA-PCR**; the antibody belongs to the mother.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO 2023: viral load classed as undetectable (zero sexual transmission), suppressed but detectable up to 1000 copies/mL (almost zero) and unsuppressed over 1000.`,`WHO 2025: long-acting cabotegravir plus rilpivirine injections recommended as a switch option for virally suppressed adults and adolescents (not yet NACO).`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - HIV and ART`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Chronic **HIV-1 (rarely HIV-2)** lentivirus infection depleting CD4 cells; **AIDS = WHO stage 4 or CD4 under 200**; **AHD = CD4 under 200 or stage 3-4, or any child under 5**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Over 18 months: **NACO Strategy III - three tests of different principles, all reactive**; discordant = indeterminate (retest 14-28 days); infants **DNA-PCR on DBS at 6 weeks, 6 and 12 months**, antibody at 18 months.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**WHO clinical stages 1-4** (no moving back down); window period 3-12 weeks (3rd gen), **2-4 weeks (4th gen p24)**, 10-14 days NAT.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`gp120 -> CD4 + **CCR5/CXCR4** -> gp41 fusion -> reverse transcription (error-prone) -> **integrase** -> protease maturation; GALT depletion -> immune activation; latent reservoir -> **ART lifelong**.`],[`Key history`,`Risk exposures, weight loss, chronic diarrhoea/fever, **4-symptom TB screen every visit**, headache (crypto), visual floaters (CMV), drug interactions, adherence, pregnancy plans, HBV/HCV, renal disease.`],[`Key examination`,`Weight/BMI, oral thrush, hairy leukoplakia, PGL, zoster scars, skin rashes, chest and SpO2 on walking, meningism, **dilated fundoscopy if CD4 under 50**, genital ulcers.`],[`Investigations`,`Baseline **CD4**, CBC, creatinine, LFT, glucose, lipids, HBsAg, anti-HCV, VDRL; CD4 under 200 - **serum CrAg and urine TB-LAM**; CBNAAT if TB screen positive; **viral load at 6, 12 months then yearly**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Treat all, same day or within 7 days**: **TLD - TDF 300 + 3TC 300 + DTG 50 mg once daily**; separate antacids/iron (2 h before, 6 h after); alternative TLE (efavirenz 400 mg).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Failure = **VL 1000 or more twice, 3 months apart, after enhanced adherence counselling**; second line **boosted PI (ATV/r or LPV/r) + optimised NRTIs (AZT + 3TC)**; third line by expert committee after genotyping.`],[`Key interactions`,`**Rifampicin - extra DTG 50 mg 12 h later** (continue 2 weeks after); PI with rifabutin; carbamazepine/phenytoin lower DTG; **cap metformin at 1000 mg/day** with DTG.`],[`OI prophylaxis and timing`,`**CPT 1 DS daily** if CD4 under 350/stage 3-4/HIV-TB; TPT 6H or 3HP; CrAg+ - fluconazole 800 -> 400 -> 200 mg; ART within 2 weeks of ATT; **defer 4-8 weeks in TB meningitis, 4-6 weeks in crypto meningitis**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy - lifelong TLD (Option B+)**, VL at 34-36 weeks, vaginal delivery if under 1000; infant **nevirapine 6 weeks (12 + AZT if high risk)**, CPT from 6 weeks; exclusive breastfeeding 6 months; children ABC/3TC + DTG by weight band.`],[`PEP and PrEP`,`**PEP: TLD x 28 days within 2 h (max 72 h)**, HIV test at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months; PrEP daily TDF/FTC (90% protection), **injectable cabotegravir 2-monthly and lenacapavir 6-monthly (WHO 2025)**.`],[`Toxicities`,`TDF - tubulopathy (avoid CrCl under 50); DTG - weight gain, insomnia, hyperglycaemia; EFV - vivid dreams; AZT - anaemia; ABC - hypersensitivity (HLA-B*5701, never rechallenge); ATV - harmless jaundice.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Meningitis features, PCP with hypoxia, visual loss, focal deficit, SJS rash, hepatitis flare after stopping TDF/3TC in HBV, positive infant DNA-PCR (start ART same day).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review 2 and 4 weeks, monthly till stable, then multi-month dispensing; yearly VL, creatinine, glucose, lipids; cervical screen at diagnosis then 3-yearly.`],[`Law and programme`,`**HIV/AIDS Act 2017** - consent, confidentiality, anti-discrimination, Ombudsman; **NACP-V, 95-95-95**; about 2.5 million PLHIV in India.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2025**: lenacapavir twice-yearly PrEP, **CAB/RPV long-acting switch** for suppressed patients; **WHO 2023**: undetectable = zero risk, under 1000 = almost zero risk.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`U=U; one high VL is non-adherence until proved otherwise; never stop TDF/3TC abruptly in HBV; IRIS - continue ART; TB is the commonest OI and killer.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`From positive test to suppressed viral load`,steps:[{label:`Three reactive tests (Strategy III)`,detail:`Consent and counselling; link to ART centre`},{label:`Baseline CD4, labs, TB screen`,detail:`CD4 under 200: CrAg and TB-LAM`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Meningitis? defer ART`,detail:`Crypto 4-6 weeks, TB meningitis 4-8 weeks`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Start TLD same day or within 7 days`,detail:`CPT if CD4 under 350; TPT once TB excluded`},{label:`Viral load at 6 and 12 months`,detail:`1000 or more: 3 months adherence counselling, repeat`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Suppressed - yearly VL, MMD`,detail:`Undetectable = untransmittable`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Occupational exposure: PEP in 4 steps`,steps:[{label:`First aid`,detail:`Wash with soap and water; flush mucosa; no squeezing`},{label:`Assess exposure and source`,detail:`Intact skin or no visible blood - no PEP`,tone:`decision`},{label:`TLD x 28 days, ideally within 2 h`,detail:`Not beyond 72 h; baseline HIV, HBV, HCV`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Follow-up tests`,detail:`HIV at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months; HBIG + vaccine if needed`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`ART drugs: the toxicity to remember`,columns:[`Drug`,`Toxicity`,`Action`],rows:[[`Tenofovir DF`,`Renal tubulopathy, bone loss`,`Avoid if CrCl under 50`],[`Dolutegravir`,`Weight gain, insomnia, hyperglycaemia`,`Double dose with rifampicin`],[`Zidovudine`,`Anaemia, neutropenia`,`Avoid if Hb under 8`],[`Abacavir`,`Hypersensitivity (HLA-B*5701)`,`Never rechallenge`],[`Efavirenz`,`Vivid dreams, depression`,`Bedtime, empty stomach`]]}]},"infectious-fever-antimicrobial-resistance":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**AMR** is a microbe's ability to survive a drug that once killed or inhibited it.`,`Bacterial AMR caused **1.27 million deaths directly** worldwide in 2019.`,`India has the **highest national AMR death toll** and very high antibiotic use.`,`Over **60% of Indian E. coli make ESBL**; about **40% of S. aureus is MRSA**.`,`**NDM-1** carbapenemase, first described in 2008, is endemic in Indian hospitals.`,`**Stewardship** means the right drug, dose, route, duration and timing.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**MDR**: non-susceptible to at least one agent in **three or more categories**.`,`**XDR**: susceptible in only **one or two categories**.`,`**PDR**: non-susceptible to **all agents in all categories**.`,`**WHO AWaRe**: Access, Watch and Reserve antibiotic groups.`,`TB differs: **MDR-TB** is isoniazid plus rifampicin resistance.`,`**Intrinsic** resistance is natural; **acquired** resistance comes by mutation or gene transfer.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Selection pressure**: each course kills susceptible flora and leaves resistant bacteria to multiply.`,`Chromosomal mutations alter targets, such as **gyrA** in typhoid and **rpoB** in TB.`,`**Plasmid conjugation** spreads resistance genes between species.`,`**ESBLs** destroy third-generation cephalosporins; **carbapenemases** (NDM, OXA-48) destroy carbapenems.`,`**mecA** makes PBP2a, so MRSA resists all beta-lactams.`,`Porin loss and **efflux pumps** cause multidrug resistance.`,`Resistance moves between humans, animals, sewage and drug factory effluent (**One Health**).`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Over-the-counter sale** is the biggest community driver.`,`**Self-medication** with leftover antibiotics and informal providers.`,`Antibiotics for **viral URI, watery diarrhoea and undiagnosed fever**.`,`**Irrational fixed-dose combinations** such as cefixime-ofloxacin.`,`Antibiotics as **growth promoters** in poultry and aquaculture.`,`Poor sanitation, weak hospital infection control and limited laboratories.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Ask: is it bacterial, does it need an antibiotic, which **narrowest Access drug**, how few days.`,`**No antibiotic** for colds, acute bronchitis, most sore throats and early sinusitis.`,`Use **back-up (delayed) prescribing** when unsure; it cuts use safely.`,`Strep throat: **amoxicillin 500 mg TDS for 10 days** or benzathine penicillin once.`,`Cystitis: **nitrofurantoin 5 days or fosfomycin 3 g once**.`,`CAP without comorbidity: **amoxicillin 1 g TDS for 5 days**.`,`Watery diarrhoea: **ORS and zinc**, not antibiotics.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Shorter courses** work: 5 days for CAP and cellulitis, 3-5 for cystitis.`,`Dose by weight and kidney function; switch **IV to oral** when stable.`,`**De-escalate** to the narrowest drug once culture returns.`,`Surgical prophylaxis: **cefazolin 2 g single dose** within 60 minutes before incision.`,`**Reserve drugs** like colistin and ceftazidime-avibactam are hospital-only, specialist-authorised.`,`NDM is not inhibited by avibactam, so **add aztreonam**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NAP-AMR 2017**: One Health plan with **six strategic priorities**.`,`**NAP-AMR 2.0 (2025-2029)** gives each ministry its own budgeted plan.`,`Kerala's **KARSAP (2018)** was the first state AMR plan.`,`**Schedule H1** register is kept for **3 years**.`,`The **Red Line** marks prescription-only antibiotic packs.`,`Colistin was **banned as an animal growth promoter in 2019** (mcr-1).`,`World AMR Awareness Week is **18-24 November**.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Hand hygiene** at the WHO five moments is the top infection-control measure.`,`Use care bundles and **remove devices early** in hospital patients.`,`Hospital AMS: local antibiogram, audit, and **pre-authorisation of Reserve drugs**.`,`Vaccines (PCV, Hib, TCV, influenza) **cut antibiotic use**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Audit yourself: antibiotics in **under 30%** of OPD encounters.`,`Target **at least 70% Access** use by 2030 (UNGA 2024).`,`Refer sepsis after repeated courses; **culture before antibiotics**.`,`Refer UTI not better at 48 hours and typhoid not settling by day 5.`,`Report clusters of neonatal or post-operative infection.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Access-first antibiotics at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin (pharyngitis)`,`500 mg oral TDS; child 40-50 mg/kg/day`,`10 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`Amoxicillin (CAP)`,`1 g oral TDS`,`5 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`Benzathine penicillin`,`1.2 million units IM; 600,000 under 27 kg`,`Single dose`,`Rare anaphylaxis; observe 30 min`],[`Nitrofurantoin`,`100 mg oral BD`,`5 days`,`Avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg oral QID`,`5 days`,`GI upset, rash`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral BD`,`5-7 days`,`Photosensitivity; not in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin (Watch)`,`500 mg-1 g oral OD`,`Dysentery 3 days; enteric 7`,`QT prolongation; specific indications only`],[`Cefazolin`,`2 g IV (3 g if over 120 kg)`,`Single dose before incision`,`Redose only if surgery over 4 h`]]},{heading:`WHO AWaRe groups`,columns:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Use`],rows:[[`Access`,`Amoxicillin, cephalexin, doxycycline, nitrofurantoin, cotrimoxazole`,`Most common infections`],[`Watch`,`Azithromycin, ciprofloxacin, cefixime, ceftriaxone, meropenem`,`Specific indications only`],[`Reserve`,`Colistin, linezolid, tigecycline, ceftazidime-avibactam`,`Hospital only, confirmed MDR`]]},{heading:`MDR, XDR and PDR`,columns:[`Type`,`Definition`,`Example`],rows:[[`MDR`,`Non-susceptible in 3 or more categories`,`ESBL E. coli`],[`XDR`,`Susceptible in only 1-2 categories`,`Acinetobacter sensitive only to colistin`],[`PDR`,`Resistant to all agents`,`Pan-resistant Klebsiella`]]}],remember:[`MDR **3 or more** categories; XDR only **1-2** left; PDR none.`,`**Access, Watch, Reserve**: at least 70% Access by 2030.`,`NAP-AMR 2017: **six priorities**, One Health; 2.0 covers 2025-2029.`,`Schedule H1 register **3 years**; Red Line on prescription-only packs.`,`Surgical prophylaxis: **one dose** of cefazolin within 60 minutes.`,`Antibiotics do not treat fever; **diagnosis does**.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`NAP-AMR 2.0 (2025-2029) launched 18 November 2025: ministry-wise budgeted plans, private-sector engagement, wastewater surveillance of AMR and residues.`,`WHO AWaRe 2025 (with EML 2025): 268 antibiotics classified; EML lists 50 antibiotics, 10 Reserve.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - AMR and stewardship`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Ability of a microbe to **survive and multiply despite a drug that once killed it**; **stewardship** = right drug, dose, route, duration, timing; IPC prevents spread.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**MDR** - non-susceptible to 1+ agent in **3 or more categories**; **XDR** - susceptible in only 1-2; **PDR** - all; TB: MDR (H+R), pre-XDR (+FQ), XDR (+bedaquiline or linezolid); **AWaRe - Access, Watch, Reserve**.`],[`Burden`,`1.27 million deaths attributable, 4.95 million associated (2019); **GRAM forecasts 39 million attributable deaths 2025-2050**; India highest toll; **ESBL E. coli over 60%, MRSA about 40%**.`],[`Mechanisms`,`Selection pressure -> mutation (gyrA, rpoB) or **plasmid transfer (conjugation)** -> beta-lactamases (**ESBL CTX-M, NDM, OXA-48**), altered target (**mecA PBP2a**, vanA), porin loss, efflux, **mcr-1 colistin**, biofilm.`],[`Drivers in India`,`**OTC sale**, self-medication, informal providers, antibiotics for viral illness, irrational FDCs, patient pressure, veterinary growth promoters, pharma effluent, poor WASH and IPC, weak labs.`],[`Priority pathogens (WHO BPPL 2024)`,`**Critical**: carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter, CR and 3GC-resistant Enterobacterales, **rifampicin-resistant TB**; high: FQ-resistant Typhi and Shigella, CR Pseudomonas, VRE, MRSA, resistant gonococcus.`],[`National strategy`,`**NAP-AMR 2017** - 6 priorities on One Health; **NAP-AMR 2.0 (2025-29)** launched 18 Nov 2025; KARSAP 2018 first state plan; NARS-Net, AMRSN, GLASS.`],[`Regulation`,`**Schedule H1** (2014) - separate register 3 years; **Red Line** campaign 2016; colistin growth-promoter ban 2019; DGHS 2024 - **write the indication** on antibiotic prescriptions.`],[`Treatment - first line (rational prescribing)`,`4 questions: bacterial? needs antibiotic? **narrowest Access drug**? **shortest duration**? - cystitis nitrofurantoin 5 d; CAP amoxicillin 1 g TDS x 5 d; cellulitis cephalexin 5 d; strep throat amoxicillin 10 d.`],[`No-antibiotic and delayed strategies`,`Common cold, bronchitis, most sore throats, sinusitis under 10 days, AOM over 2 years; **back-up prescription** (use if worse at 3-5 days); watery diarrhoea - ORS and zinc.`],[`Treatment - escalation (Reserve drugs)`,`Reserve drugs (colistin, ceftazidime-avibactam, tigecycline, linezolid for MDR) **hospital-only, pre-authorised**; NDM - ceftazidime-avibactam **plus aztreonam**.`],[`Hospital stewardship (ICMR AMSP 2018)`,`AMS committee, local antibiogram, prescription audit, pre-authorisation, IV-to-oral switch, de-escalation; **surgical prophylaxis cefazolin 2 g single dose within 60 min**.`],[`Infection control`,`**Hand hygiene at 5 moments** (alcohol rub 20-30 s), device bundles, early device removal, biomedical waste segregation, Kayakalp/NQAS.`],[`Indicators`,`OPD encounters with antibiotic **under 30%**, **Access share 60% now, 70% by 2030**, DOT/DDD per 1000 patient-days, HAI rates.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Sepsis after several courses (MDR - culture first), UTI not better at 48 h, typhoid not settling by day 5, suspected DR-TB, neonatal or post-op infection clusters.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NAP-AMR 2.0** launched Nov 2025 (20+ ministries, wastewater surveillance); **WHO AWaRe 2025** (268 antibiotics); **UNGA 2024** - 10% fewer AMR deaths by 2030, 70% Access.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Antibiotics do not treat fever - diagnosis does; WAAW 18-24 November; Chennai Declaration 2012; NDM not inhibited by avibactam; vaccines cut antibiotic use.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Before writing any antibiotic in the OPD`,steps:[{label:`Is it bacterial?`,detail:`Cold, bronchitis, most sore throats, watery diarrhoea - no`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Self-limiting - no or back-up prescription`,detail:`Safety-net; use only if worse at 3-5 days`,tone:`good`},{label:`Needs antibiotic - narrowest Access drug`,detail:`Amoxicillin, cephalexin, nitrofurantoin, doxycycline`},{label:`Shortest effective course`,detail:`5 days CAP and cellulitis; 5 days cystitis`},{label:`Culture first if complicated or failing`,detail:`De-escalate when result returns; write the indication`},{label:`Audit: antibiotic rate under 30%, Access 70%`,detail:`Compare with peers yearly`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`WHO AWaRe groups`,root:`Antibiotic classification for stewardship`,arms:[{label:`Access`,tone:`good`,steps:[`Amoxicillin, cephalexin, cloxacillin`,`Doxycycline, nitrofurantoin, cotrimoxazole`,`First choice; 70% of use by 2030`]},{label:`Watch`,tone:`decision`,steps:[`Azithromycin, cefixime, ceftriaxone`,`Ciprofloxacin, pip-taz, meropenem`,`Specific indications only`]},{label:`Reserve`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Colistin, polymyxin B`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam, tigecycline, linezolid`,`Hospital, pre-authorised, confirmed MDR`]}]}]},"infectious-fever-tropical-fevers-zoonoses":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Zoonoses** are infections naturally transmitted between vertebrate animals and humans.`,`About **60% of known and 75% of emerging** human infections are zoonotic.`,`Key Indian zoonoses: **leptospirosis, scrub typhus, JE, KFD, Nipah, brucellosis, anthrax, plague**.`,`**AES** is acute fever with altered mental status or new seizures.`,`**One Health** links human, animal and environmental health.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Direct zoonoses**: one vertebrate host, such as rabies, brucellosis, anthrax.`,`**Cyclozoonoses**: two vertebrate hosts, such as hydatid and Taenia.`,`**Metazoonoses**: need an invertebrate vector, such as plague, JE, KFD, scrub typhus.`,`**Saprozoonoses**: soil or plant reservoir, such as larva migrans.`,`By direction: anthropozoonoses, zooanthroponoses and amphixenoses.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Leptospira** in rat or cattle urine enters through **abraded skin** from water.`,`Leptospiral vasculitis causes **calf tenderness, conjunctival suffusion**, jaundice and AKI.`,`**Weil disease**: deep jaundice, **non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI** and bleeding.`,`**Chikungunya** alphavirus infects synovium, causing severe symmetric polyarthralgia.`,`**JE**: Culex mosquitoes; **pigs amplify**, ardeid birds maintain, humans are dead-end hosts.`,`**KFD**: tick-borne, **monkey deaths** are the sentinel; first seen in Shimoga, 1957.`,`**Nipah**: fruit bats; spreads by contaminated fruit or palm sap, then **person to person**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **occupation**: paddy farming, sewage work, dairy, abattoir, forest work.`,`Ask about **floodwater or wading** in the last 2-4 weeks, and rats.`,`Ask about **raw milk** (brucellosis) and handling a **dead animal** (anthrax).`,`Ask about **rat falls** (plague), pigs (JE), bats or palm sap (Nipah).`,`Ask about forest visits and **monkey deaths** (KFD).`,`Ask about clusters, pregnancy, and JE vaccination.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Conjunctival suffusion plus calf tenderness** strongly suggests leptospirosis.`,`Search for an **eschar**: scrub typhus; painless eschar with massive oedema: anthrax.`,`Look for icterus, bleeding, oliguria and hypotension.`,`Check SpO2; haemoptysis suggests **leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage**.`,`Small-joint swelling suggests chikungunya; sacroiliac tenderness suggests brucellosis.`,`A **painful bubo** in groin or axilla suggests plague.`,`Check **capillary glucose** in every child with AES.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`First test for **malaria and dengue**, with CBC, LFT, creatinine and urine.`,`Leptospirosis: **IgM ELISA after day 5**; **MAT** is the gold standard.`,`Chikungunya: **RT-PCR on days 1-5**, IgM after day 5.`,`JE: **IgM MAC-ELISA in CSF**; also test for scrub typhus.`,`Nipah and KFD: **RT-PCR at NIV Pune** with full biosafety.`,`Brucellosis: prolonged blood culture and **SAT 1:160 or more**.`,`**Do not wait for serology** to treat a suspected bacterial zoonosis.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Paracetamol and fluids; **no NSAIDs until dengue is excluded**.`,`Mild leptospirosis: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days**.`,`Alternatives: amoxicillin for 7 days, or **azithromycin 500 mg for 3 days** in pregnancy.`,`Malaria and dengue negative, no focus: empirical **doxycycline** covers scrub and lepto.`,`Chikungunya: **paracetamol** first; no steroids in the acute phase.`,`Viral zoonoses (JE, KFD, Nipah, chikungunya) need **supportive care only**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Severe leptospirosis: **IV penicillin G or ceftriaxone** for 7 days.`,`Watch for a **Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction** after the first dose.`,`Weil disease: replace potassium and start **early dialysis** when needed.`,`Brucellosis: **doxycycline 6 weeks plus gentamicin 7 days**, or plus rifampicin.`,`Plague: **gentamicin or streptomycin**; contacts get doxycycline for 7 days.`,`Cutaneous anthrax: ciprofloxacin or doxycycline 7-10 days; **never incise the eschar**.`,`Chronic chikungunya arthritis: **hydroxychloroquine** or methotrexate under rheumatology.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Jaundice with oliguria or bleeding after floodwater: **Weil disease**.`,`Haemoptysis in leptospirosis: pulmonary haemorrhage, **mortality over 50%**.`,`Child with fever and seizures: **AES**; check glucose and refer.`,`Encephalitis cluster with bat exposure: **suspect Nipah**; isolate and notify.`,`Painful bubo or rapid pneumonia in a rat-fall area: **plague**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Weekly **doxycycline 200 mg** protects during flood relief or paddy planting.`,`**JE vaccine SA 14-14-2** at **9-12 and 16-24 months** in endemic districts.`,`In plague, **kill the fleas before the rats**.`,`Gumboots and gloves for farmers; **boiled or pasteurised milk**.`,`Nipah: avoid bat-bitten fruit and raw palm sap; monitor contacts **21 days**.`,`Notify on **IHIP using S, P and L forms**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Zoonosis drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Doxycycline (lepto, scrub)`,`100 mg oral BD; child 2.2 mg/kg`,`7 days`,`Photosensitivity; not in pregnancy`],[`Doxycycline (prophylaxis)`,`200 mg oral once weekly`,`During exposure`,`Start 1-2 days before`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg oral OD`,`3 days lepto; 5 days scrub`,`QT prolongation; choice in pregnancy`],[`Penicillin G or ceftriaxone`,`1.5 MU IV 6-hourly; ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV OD`,`7 days`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction`],[`Doxycycline + gentamicin (brucella)`,`100 mg BD + gentamicin 5 mg/kg IV OD`,`6 weeks; gentamicin 7 days`,`Nephro- and ototoxicity`],[`Gentamicin (plague)`,`5 mg/kg IV OD`,`10 days`,`Check creatinine`],[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg 6-8-hourly, max 3-4 g/day`,`While symptomatic`,`First choice in all tropical fevers`],[`Naproxen`,`250-500 mg oral BD`,`Subacute chikungunya arthritis`,`Only after dengue excluded`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`200-400 mg oral daily`,`Chronic chikungunya arthritis`,`Retinopathy; eye checks`]]},{heading:`Fever clues: which zoonosis?`,columns:[`Disease`,`Clue`,`Test`],rows:[[`Leptospirosis`,`Floodwater, calf tenderness, red eyes, jaundice with low-K AKI`,`IgM ELISA; MAT`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar, nodes, post-monsoon`,`IgM ELISA, eschar PCR`],[`Chikungunya`,`Severe symmetric joint pain, rash`,`RT-PCR day 1-5; IgM later`],[`Brucellosis`,`Raw milk, sweats, sacroiliitis, orchitis`,`Blood culture, SAT`],[`JE / AES`,`Fever with confusion or seizures, paddy area`,`CSF JE IgM`],[`KFD`,`Forest, monkey deaths, bleeding`,`RT-PCR`]]},{heading:`Zoonoses by transmission cycle`,columns:[`Type`,`Hosts`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Direct`,`One vertebrate host`,`Rabies, brucellosis, anthrax, leptospirosis`],[`Cyclozoonoses`,`Two vertebrate hosts`,`Hydatid, Taenia solium`],[`Metazoonoses`,`Vertebrate plus vector`,`Plague, JE, KFD, scrub typhus`],[`Saprozoonoses`,`Soil or plant reservoir`,`Larva migrans`]]}],remember:[`**Red eyes plus calf tenderness** after floodwater: leptospirosis.`,`Lepto AKI is **non-oliguric with low potassium**; jaundice deep, enzymes modest.`,`**MAT** is the lepto gold standard; doxycycline 200 mg weekly prevents it.`,`JE: **pigs amplify**, birds maintain, humans are dead-end hosts.`,`AES: always **check sugar** and cover scrub typhus.`,`KFD: Shimoga 1957; **monkey deaths** are the warning.`,`Plague: **kill fleas before rats**.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO 2025 arboviral guideline: no NSAIDs while dengue is possible and no corticosteroids in acute chikungunya; prednisolone kept only for disabling subacute tenosynovitis.`,`FDA 2025: live chikungunya vaccine Ixchiq licence suspended (August 2025); VLP vaccine Vimkunya approved for 12 years and over; none in India's programme.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Leptospirosis, chikungunya, JE/AES, zoonoses`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Zoonoses** - infections naturally transmitted between vertebrate animals and humans (60% of known, 75% of emerging); **AES** = fever + altered mental status or new seizures.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By cycle: **direct** (rabies, brucellosis, anthrax), **cyclozoonoses** (hydatid, Taenia), **metazoonoses** (plague, JE, KFD, scrub), **saprozoonoses** (larva migrans); by direction - anthropozoonoses, zooanthroponoses, amphixenoses.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Floodwater, rats (lepto), raw milk (brucella), dead animals (anthrax), pigs and paddy (JE), **monkey deaths in forest (KFD)**, bat-bitten fruit and palm sap (Nipah), rat falls (plague), Aedes (chikungunya).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Leptospira via abraded skin -> **capillary vasculitis** -> suffusion, calf myositis -> Weil (jaundice, **non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI**, pulmonary haemorrhage); chikungunya alphavirus -> synovitis -> chronic arthritis; JE - pigs amplify, humans dead-end.`],[`Key history`,`Occupation, floodwater 2-4 weeks, animal contact, forest visits, fever pattern (disabling joint pain, red eyes, undulant fever), clusters, JE vaccination, pregnancy, litchi and missed meals (AES child).`],[`Key examination`,`**Conjunctival suffusion + calf tenderness** (lepto), eschar (scrub; anthrax with massive oedema), icterus, oliguria, SpO2, symmetric small-joint swelling, bubo, sacroiliac tenderness, GCS and **glucose in AES**.`],[`Investigations`,`Malaria and dengue first; lepto - low K, high bilirubin, raised CK, **IgM ELISA after day 5, MAT gold standard (1:400)**; chikungunya RT-PCR day 1-5, IgM after; **JE IgM in CSF**; Nipah/KFD RT-PCR at NIV.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Mild lepto: **doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days** (amoxicillin or azithromycin 500 mg x 3 days in pregnancy); chikungunya: **paracetamol, no NSAID till dengue excluded, no acute steroids**; empirical AUFI - doxycycline.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Severe lepto: **penicillin G 1.5 MU 6-hourly or ceftriaxone 1-2 g x 7 days** (Jarisch-Herxheimer), dialysis, ventilation; chronic chikungunya - **HCQ 200-400 mg** or methotrexate.`],[`Other zoonoses`,`**Brucellosis**: doxycycline 6 weeks + gentamicin 5 mg/kg x 7 days (or rifampicin); **anthrax**: cipro or doxy 7-10 days, never incise; **plague**: gentamicin or streptomycin 10 days, contacts doxycycline 7 days.`],[`AES management`,`ABC, **glucose (10% dextrose 2-5 mL/kg)**, seizures (midazolam then levetiracetam), raised ICP, **empirical doxycycline/azithromycin + acyclovir + ceftriaxone**; refer to AES centre.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - azithromycin or amoxicillin not doxycycline; children under 8 with brucellosis - cotrimoxazole + rifampicin; Nipah - strict isolation, PPE.`],[`Complications`,`Weil disease, **leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage (over 50% mortality)**, JE sequelae (one-third), KFD haemorrhage and meningoencephalitis, chronic chikungunya arthritis, brucella spondylitis.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Jaundice + oliguria or bleeding after floodwater, haemoptysis, AES, encephalitis/respiratory cluster with bat exposure (Nipah), monkey deaths + fever (KFD), bubo, black eschar with oedema.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review at 48-72 h on doxycycline; lepto creatinine and K every 24-48 h; brucellosis for 1 year (relapse); chikungunya arthritis at 1 and 3 months; JE survivors - rehabilitation.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Doxycycline 200 mg weekly** lepto prophylaxis; rodent control (kill fleas before rats in plague); **JE SA 14-14-2 at 9-12 and 16-24 months (UIP)**; KFD - DMP oil; Nipah - 21-day contact monitoring; IHIP S-P-L forms; One Health.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2025** arboviral guideline - no NSAIDs or steroids in acute chikungunya; **2025** Ixchiq licence suspended (US), Vimkunya approved; no chikungunya vaccine in India's programme.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Suffusion + calf tenderness = lepto; pigs amplify JE; KFD Shimoga 1957; kill fleas before rats; AES - always check sugar and cover scrub.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Monsoon fever after floodwater: leptospirosis pathway`,steps:[{label:`Fever, myalgia, red eyes after floodwater`,detail:`Calf tenderness, conjunctival suffusion`},{label:`Exclude malaria and dengue`,detail:`RDT/smear, NS1; CBC, creatinine, K, bilirubin, CK`},{label:`Jaundice, oliguria, bleeding or hypoxia?`,detail:`Weil disease or pulmonary haemorrhage`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No - doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days`,detail:`Azithromycin in pregnancy; review 48-72 h`,tone:`good`},{label:`Yes - IV penicillin G or ceftriaxone`,detail:`K replacement, early dialysis, ventilation`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Confirm and notify`,detail:`IgM after day 5, MAT; report on IHIP`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Tropical fever look-alikes`,columns:[`Disease`,`Clue`,`Test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Leptospirosis`,`Floodwater, calves, low K AKI`,`IgM, MAT`,`Doxycycline; severe penicillin G`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar, nodes`,`IgM, eschar PCR`,`Doxycycline 7 days`],[`Chikungunya`,`Disabling symmetric arthralgia`,`RT-PCR, IgM`,`Paracetamol; HCQ if chronic`],[`Dengue`,`Rising Hct, falling platelets`,`NS1, IgM`,`Fluids; no NSAID`],[`Brucellosis`,`Raw milk, sacroiliitis`,`Culture, SAT 1:160`,`Doxycycline 6 wk + gentamicin`]]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Child with fever and altered sensorium (AES)`,steps:[{label:`Airway, breathing, circulation`,detail:`Oxygen, position, IV access`},{label:`Capillary glucose within minutes`,detail:`Correct hypoglycaemia with 10% dextrose 2-5 mL/kg`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Control seizures`,detail:`Midazolam, then levetiracetam or phenytoin`},{label:`Empirical cover`,detail:`Doxycycline/azithromycin + acyclovir + ceftriaxone`,tone:`decision`},{label:`CSF and serum JE IgM, scrub IgM`,detail:`Refer to district AES centre; notify`,tone:`good`}]}]}};export{e as default};